      {
        "finding_id": "section-25c56fe1651f86bc0725",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the mean time, Turnley and his companions \nmade a much better shift than they had done before, \nhis friend Redding’s present being of infinite use to \nthem, for they constantly kept a good fire, with which \nthey broiled their fish. There were plenty of land \ncrabs and snakes on the island, which they could eat \nwhen they were dressed. Thus they passed fourteen \ndays; at the end of which the pirates made them an- \nother visit, and they according to custom made for the \nwoods, thinking that the reason of their return must \nbe, in order to force them to serve amongst them. \nBut here they were mistaken, for the anger of these \nfellows being over, they began to pity them; but going \nashore, and not finding them, they knew they were \nhid for fear. Nevertheless, they left upon the shore, \nwhere they knew they would come, some stores which \nthey intended in this fit of good humour to present \nthem with.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
          "source_file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 52758,
          "end_char": 53669,
