      {
        "finding_id": "section-fe0324243871764916bc",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Meanwhile the pirates, having returned to the \nisland, were deep in a great carouse to celebrate the \nrich haul which they had so easily made. Much \nheady wine was drunk, boastful speeches were made, \nand song and jest sped the pleasant time. Even the \nlook-out men on the highest point of the rocks had \njoined in the festivity and no watch was kept upon \nthe sea. When, therefore, with the suddenness of a \ntempest out of the summer sky men rushed upon them \nfrom behind the rocks the half-drunken pirates were \nable to make but little resistance against what were \nfound to be overwhelming numbers. Those who at- \ntempted to fight were cut down; the others were sur- \nrounded and ordered to throw down their arms.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
