      {
        "finding_id": "section-0188c3019e0cbb17ab07",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The China New Year was approaching (February), \na great time with all Chinamen—a general holiday— \na feast time—a time that business is thrown aside, and \nrevelry and dissipation are alone thought of. Even \nthe pirates cannot resist the temptation of general lax- \nity, and as a rule return to some rendezvous or strong- \nhold for at least three days. Another custom, and a \nvery good one, connected with their New Year is, \nthat every Chinaman pays his debts; it is a point of \nhonour with them to do so; an item in the general \nroutine of a Chinaman’s life we might well imitate. \nRelying on this general slackness, I had decided to \ncruise down the west coast during their holiday-time, \nhoping to make a good bag. ‘The day before the com- \nmencement of their New Year, 1876, I visited some \nChinese merchants, and talked over the state of trade, \npiracy, etc., but none had any news such as I wanted. \nAs I was in the act of getting under weigh, one of \nthese same men came quietly on board, and in a mys- \nterious manner whispered—‘Better look see Puck- \nshui.”’",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
