      {
        "finding_id": "section-56495b803de73b3ed2e9",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "There was, of course, no possibility of bringing the \npirates to bay, and nothing was left to be done but to \nreturn on board. I was just about ordering the men \nto fall in, when, on looking down the creek, to my no \nlittle astonishment, a whole fleet of junks appeared \nsteering in. The Chinese interpreter immediately \npronounced them to be pirates. Pleasant, I thought; \nwhy, they will take the gun-boat long before we can \nget on board. My telescope, however, revealed that \nthey were all the same class of craft, a thing never the \ncase in a fleet of piratical junks; Mandarins I felt \nsure, from their uniformity and number of flags flying. \nHowever, to make things certain, I got quickly down \nto the boats, and pulled out for the headmost craft, \nhailing her as I came near as to her friendliness or \notherwise. This proved the commander-in-chief’s \njunk, whom I requested to come on board the gun- \nboat, and returned myself to receive him. I shall \nnever forget the man’s face as he reached the deck.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
