      {
        "finding_id": "section-cb2aca21058cf7bcc241",
        "file": "greatpiratesstor0000jose_greatpiratesstor0000jose_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Great pirate stories. Second series",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/greatpiratesstor0000jose",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "1925-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "97e06b715ec15879e9da76b05cd5250edc5df0b287c0736c121fbebff2f7fc41",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The part I chiefly had occasion to visit appeared in- \nhabited by two tribes, the Hacka’s and Punti’s, who \nby no means lived at peace with one another,—quite \nthe contrary. They were always fighting or cutting \neach other’s throats on a small scale, as well as by \nmore wholesale operations. I had on one occasion to \nfollow a lorcha and a couple of junks up a sluggish \nriver which ran through this country, and the amount \nof fighting we passed through was absurd. Neither \nparty molested us in any way, although, if so disposed, \nthey might have made it very disagreeable, the width \nof the river being only at most sixty yards, and the \nbanks here and there well bushed over. Dead bodies \nin scores floated down, or were grounded on the \nbanks. The hills on either side of the river were \nquite decorated with the flags of the contending par- \nties; but it must be understood that these emblems of \nwarfare in a Chinese army, or in a tribal squabble, in- \nvariably are almost as plentiful as the men themselves. \nThe three pirate crafts were captured and destroyed.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
