      {
        "finding_id": "section-65f11ad1ddd631fc789b",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In all the ages of which we have any record there \nhave been men who gained a living by that practice \nof robbery on the high seas which we know by the \nname of Piracy. Perhaps the pirates best known \nto the English-speaking world are the buccaneers \nof the Spanish Main, who flourished exceedingly \nin the seventeenth century, and of whom many \nchronicles exist: principally owing to the labours \nof that John Esquemelin, a pirate of a literary \nturn of mind, who added the crime of authorship \nto the ill deeds of a sea-rover. The Sea-Wolves \nof the Mediterranean in the preceding century \ndid not raise up a chronicler from among them- \nselves: for not much tincture of learning seems \nto have distinguished these desperate fighters and \naccomplished seamen, descendants of those Spanish \nMoslems who had, during the Middle Ages, lived \nin a land in which learning and culture had been \nheld in the highest estimation. Driven from their \nhomes, their civilisation crushed, their religion \nbanned in that portion of Southern Spain in which \nthey had dwelt for over seven centuries, cast upon \nthe shores of Northern Africa, these men took to \n1",
