      {
        "finding_id": "section-33ea4d539b951c1b9b3f",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "We shall speak, later on, of the autocratic rule \nof these leaders who possessed so absolute a \ndomination over the men by whom they were \nfollowed. The fact of this absolute supremacy on \nthe part of the chiefs is very curious, as theo- \nretically in the confederacy of the Sea-wolves all \nwere equal; we are, in fact, confronted with pure \ndemocracy, where every man was at liberty to do \nwhat seemed best in his own eyes. He was a free \nagent, none coercing him or desiring him to place \nhimself under discipline or command. This, be it \nobserved, was the theory. As a matter of fact the \ncorsairs, who were extraordinarily successful in \ntheir abominable trade, abode beneath an iron and \nrigid discipline. This was enforced by the lash, \nas we shall see later on when it is related how \nKheyr-ed-Din Barbarossa flogged one Hassan, a \ncaptain who, he considered, had failed in his duty : \nor by the actual penalty of death, which Uruj \nBarbarossa inflicted on one who had dared to act \nindependently of his authority.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
