      {
        "finding_id": "section-958115bbf7efaa2b71d2",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "When “the Grand Period” came to an end, as \nit did about the date already indicated, the corsairs \nhad become a permanent institution; they re- \nmained established at Algiers, Tunis, and other \nports on the littoral of Northern Africa as a recog- \nnised evil. Pirates they remained to the end of \nthe chapter, the scourge of the tideless sea; but \nno longer did they array themselves in line of battle \nagainst the mightiest potentates of the earth allied \nfor their complete destruction. It was the men of \nthe sea who set up this empire; it was they who \ndefied Charles V., a whole succession of Popes, \nAndrea Doria and his descendants, the might of \nSpain, Venice, Genoa, Catalonia, and France. It \nwas they who taught the so-called civilised world of \nthe age in which they lived that sea-power can only \nbe met and checked by those who dispose of navies \nmanned by seamen; that against it the master of \nthe mightiest legions of the land is powerless,",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
