        "finding_id": "section-5b9896165d2c8ac1f425",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "From Reggio to Genoa, from Venice to Taranto, \nthe cry of rage and fear went up ; it was re-echoed \nfrom the coasts of France and of the Balearic Islands, \nwhile Southern Spain seethed with disaffection, \nand the Moriscoes, as those Moors who remained \nin the country were known, were ever on the look- \nout to assist their bold brethren, the rovers of the \nsea, Christendom was completely bewildered : \nhitherto the relations between the nations and the \nKings of Tunis, Tlemcen, Fez, and others of the \nNorth African potentates, had been of the most \nagreeable description. Both parties had denounced \npiracy, and had as far as in them lay done all in \ntheir power to discourage this form of robbery. \nBut now all was changed, and, as has been said \nin the previous chapter, a situation arose analogous \nto that of the Spaniards in the West Indies a \ncentury and « half later when Morgan and the \nbuccaneers were at the height of their maleficent \nprowess. The situation was analogous, but whereas \nMorgan, Scott, L’Ollonais, and others terrorised \nonly such forces as Spain possessed in far-distant \ncolonies, the corsairs were a terror to all the great \nnations of the world.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
