      {
        "finding_id": "section-650312fe1b6c8bf76a45",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the meanwhile who so happy as Abu-Abd- \nAllah-Mahomed ? We cannot do better than to \ntake the description of his position from the pages \nof the good Padre Alberto Guglielmotti. The \nFranciscan says: “ He [that is, Abd-Allah] desired \npeace with all and prosperity for his own interests. \nFriendly to the merchants in their commerce ; \nfriendly to the corsairs in their spoils. Let all \nhold by the law: the former contentedly paying \ncustoms dues, the latter cheerfully handing over \na fifth part of their robberies, and Abd-Allah— \ntheir common friend—would ever continue at \npeace with them all. Outside his ports the \nmerchants and the pirates might fall by the ears if \nthey would: that was no reason for him to trouble \nhis head. On the contrary, he would joyfully await \nthem on their return either with customs dues or \ntribute of the fifth as the case might be.”",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
