      {
        "finding_id": "section-4be3f113ce49a5f530f3",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Who, it was asked in Constantinople, was this \nman who had been called in to command the \nships of the Ottomans at sea? They answered \ntheir own question, and said that he was a lawless \nman, a corsair: were there not good seamen and \nvaliant men-at-arms like the Bashas Zay and \nHimeral, who should be preferred before him; \nthis man who had come from the ends of the \nearth, and of whom nobody knew anything good ? \nAgain, could he be trusted? Something of the \nhistory of the Barbarossas had penetrated to the \ncapital of Turkey, and it was known that scrupu- \nlous adherence to their engagements had not \nalways characterised the brothers: who should \nsay that he might not carry off the galleys of the \nGrand Turk on some marauding expedition de- \nsigned for his own aggrandisement? There was \nyet more to be urged against him: not only was \nhe infamous in character, but he was no true \nMussulman, for had not his father been a mere \nrenegado, and—worst of all—had not his mother \nbeen a Christian woman ?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
