      {
        "finding_id": "section-3ecf142b5c7ef3309c0f",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "swept into the town and commenced to slay, to \nplunder, and to ravish, without distinction of age, \nsex, or nationality. Ostensibly these Christian \nwarriors had come to rescue the inhabitants of \nTunis from the oppression of Barbarossa, but while \nthat chieftain was in full flight across the mountains \nto Bona, those by whom he had been defeated \nentered the town, which they had come to save, \nand perpetrated a massacre so awful that it is said \nthat no less than thirty thousand people perished. \nIt is a terrible blot on the escutcheon of the \nEmperor; as, although he and his generals de- \nprecated the massacre—and indeed to do them \njustice tried to prevent it—this is no excuse for \nallowing their men to get out of hand, when they \nmust have been aware of the inevitable result: as \nthe Moslem corsairs at their worst were equalled in \ntheir iniquities by the European soldiery, once the \nstrong hand of discipline had relaxed its grip.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
