        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It may have been that the Emperor was dis- \npleased with this excess of zeal on the part of his \narmy ; but, if it were so, the chroniclers are silent \nconcerning the matter, being far too busy singing \nthe praises of the Cesar to think of such a trifle as \nthe massacre of most of the persons whom he had \ncome to deliver. The wretched inhabitants of \nTunis must have found it somewhat difficult to \ndistinguish between the corsair, who killed three \nthousand of their fellow townsmen, and the Christian \nEmperor, who had massacred ten times that \nnumber. Charles, however, reaped great glory \nfrom an expedition which had but one good result, \nwhich was, that he succeeded in rescuing twenty \nthousand captives ; these men, very naturally, on",
