      {
        "finding_id": "section-e9f4ae1b50425701da5f",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "for control of the wild spirits of the age. Nor was \nthis ascendaney by any means easy to obtain, as \nthe rank and file led lives of incredible bitterness, \nalmost inconceivable to modern ideas. What they \nsuffered they alone knew, but it was compounded \nof hunger, thirst, heat, cold, sickness unrelieved by \ncare or tending, wounds which festered for lack of \nmedicaments, death which ever stared them in the \nface, and last, and worst of all, the risk of capture \nby some Christian foe, by whom they would be \nchained to the rowers’ bench and taste of a bitterness \nabsolutely unimaginable. As a set-off to this the \nman who aspired to lead must offer to his followers \nat least a record of success in small things ; also \nhe had to be something of an enthusiast, something \nof an orator, some one subtly persuasive. Against \nall the disagreeables of the strenuous life of the \ncorsair he had to hold before the dazzled eyes of \nSelim, Ali, or Mahomet the promise of fat captures \nof the merchant vessels of the foe; when they had \nbut to slit a few throats and to return with their \nbrigantines laden to the gunwale with desirable \nplunder. Again he had to hearten them for possible \nencounters with Spaniards, with the terrible Doria, \nor worst of all with the dreaded Knights of St. John \nthemselves ; to point out that to die in conflict with \nthe infidel was a sure passport to heaven and its \nhouris, and to invoke great names, such as that of \nBarbarossa to show to what dizzy heights the \nfighting Moslem could climb. In such an age and \namong such men as these it was no mean feat to \nbecome a leader by whom men swore and to whom \nthey yielded a ready obedience.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
