      {
        "finding_id": "section-e4873fa6b102a24f699f",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "the part of the historian; at the same time, when \nDragut was at the height of his activities there is \nno doubt that any one passing through those seas \nran a great risk of capture; so much so in fact \nthat at this period, from 1538, the date of the \nbattle of Prevesa, until Lepanto in 1571, all \nmaritime commerce in the Mediterranean was \ngreatly circumscribed. At the beginning of this \nepoch, which saw the rise of the Moslem corsairs, \nthese robbers perforce confined themselves more \nto the North African coast than was the case later \non. The pioneers of the piratical movement, after \nthe fatal date 1492, which saw the wholesale \nexpulsion of the Moors from Spain, were com- \nparatively speaking inexpert practitioners in the \nart and mystery of piracy; they had not the habit \nof the sea, and in consequence confined their \ndepredations to the neighbourhood of their own \nselected ports in Africa, which dominated that \nsea lane running east and west through the \nMediterranean, which then, as now, was one of \nthe greatest highways of commerce of the world. \nGradually, as we have seen, under the able guidance \nof the two Barbarossas, but particularly that of the \nsecond and greater of the two, piracy became a \ncommonplace in the north, as well as in the south, \nof the tideless sea; the corsairs, as time went on, \neven devoting more time and attention to the \ncoast of Italy and the islands of the archipelago \nthan they did to the recognised trade routes. \nThese latter had become by 1540 similar to an \nestate which has been shot over too frequently ; \nbirds had become both wild and scarce, it was \nhardly worth while to go over the ground, except",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
          "source_file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
