      {
        "finding_id": "section-9cdbb2dff258173ad003",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Dracut had made it a practice never to appear in \nthe harbour of “ Africa” in any great force, as he \nhad no desire to frighten the birds whom he desired \nto snare; on the occasion of which we are now \nspeaking he had but two galleys, and their departure \nfrom the outer harbour passed almost unnoticed, \nas the ruck of the population were accustomed to \nvisits from the corsairs, who came to fill up with \nprovisions and fresh water. Swiftly as hawks his \nvessels swept along the coast collecting the garrisons \nof Susa, Sfax, and Monastir to aid him in his latest \ndesign ; they were all picked men and singularly \napt for the stern business which their leader destined \nthem to undertake. In this manner he soon col- \nlected five hundred of the stoutest and most reckless \nfighters who sailed out of the ports of Northern \nAfrica, and, when it became noised abroad among \nthem what the service was for which they were \nrequired, there was universal joy and eagerness. \nTrue the adventure was a formidable one: to \ncapture “ Africa” was no light task, even for such",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
