      {
        "finding_id": "section-a532cf05af8f2d2f2065",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Once again had a Moslem corsair bid defiance \nto that ruler whom Sandoval and Marmol in their \nhistories greet by the name of the “ Modern Cesar.” \nIt was told to Charles that Susa, Sfax, and Monastir \nhad fallen, that “ Africa” was in the hands of the \ncorsairs; “was he never to be free from these \npestilent knaves,” he demanded of his trembling \ncourtiers? Hot-foot came the couriers from \nCharles to Andrea Doria, with orders to take \nDragut dead or alive, but alive for choice; and \nup and down the tideless sea in the summer of \n1549 did the great Genoese seaman range in search \nof the bold corsair. Doria was getting a very old \nman now, but his eye was undimmed, his strength \nyet tireless, his vigilance and zeal in the service of \nhis master unabated.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
