      {
        "finding_id": "section-8aaf3b0f77722fca438a",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Having thus restored order to the finances, the \nGrand Master turned his attention to the state \nof affairs (as he had received them from his pre- \ndecessor) connected with the territorial possessions \nof the Knights. For long years now the fortress \nof Tripoli had been in the hands of the renowned \nDragut, who was the scourge and the terror of the \nChristians. The corsair dwelt in his stronghold in \ninsolent defiance of the Knights, whose property \nit once had been. Years before he had wrested it \nfrom them by the strong hand: what, then, more \nnecessary in the eyes of such an one as La Valette \nthan to expel this audacious pirate? The Grand \nMaster invited the co-operation of Juan la Cerda \n(a Spanish Grandee, Duke of Medina-Celi, and \nViceroy of Sicily for the King of Spain) in this \nenterprise. The Viceroy joyfully acceded to the \nrequest, and informed his master. Philip II. ap- \nproved the project, and sent orders to the Duke \nof Sesse, Governor of Milan, to the Duke of Alcala, \nGovernor of Naples, and to John Andrea Doria, \nGeneral of the Galleys, to join forces and to \nrepair to Sicily, placing themselves under the \norders of the Duke of Medina-Celi, who was ex- \npressly charged to take no action save by the \nadvice of the Grand Master. The expedition \nassembled, the Duke took it to Malta, where it \nwintcred, and in the spring it sailed and attacked \nTripoli.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
