      {
        "finding_id": "section-6576f588a5f9361be40b",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "La Valette, though he mourned over the repulse \nof the Christian forces from Tripoli, did not on \nthat account allow his pursuit of the infidel to \ngrow faint; the galleys of “the Religion” were \nalways at sea, and both the corsairs and the Otto- \nman Turks were perpetually losing valuable ships \nand costly merchandise. Under the General of \nthe Galleys, the Commandeur Gozon de Melac, \nand that celebrated chevalier, the Commandeur \nde Romegas, the sea forces of the Knights were \neverywhere in evidence. Into the hands of the \nChristians fell the Pefion de Velez, situated on the \nnorthern coast of Africa opposite to Malaga—a",
        "years_in_scope": [],
