      {
        "finding_id": "section-164d21a836fac4a9f606",
        "file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-Wolves of the Mediterranean: The Grand Period of the Moslem Corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, E. Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1910-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "02bcc5c0781b01bf6c6e78ee0d5b8bfb31a02ea35b47565b888e171e88ef6ad9",
        "evidence_excerpt": "such a man as Ali should have considered it a \ntriumph worth the celebrating when he defeated \nfive of their vessels with twenty-four of his own. \nThe next occurrence in the life of Ali was one \nof those to which the Sea-wolves were subjected \nfrom time to time, and which do not seem to \nhave caused them much trouble or anxiety. This \nwas a mutiny of the Janissaries in Algiers, who \nvery reasonably objected to being left without \ntheir pay. A mutiny of the Janissaries, however, \nwas somewhat a serious matter, as they were \naccustomed to the enjoyment of many privileges, \nand were, as we have said elsewhere, a picked \ncorps who had it in their power even to coerce \nthe Sultan himself upon occasions. \nThose of them who were in Algiers demanded \n““Who was this corsair who dared to keep the \npicked men of the army of the Grand Turk waiting \nfor their pay, as if they were no better than his \nslaves?” Such a thing as a mutiny was, in the \ndays of which we speak, a matter for which any \nprudent corsair had to be prepared. Ali was in \nno means discomposed, and, as the crisis had be- \ncome acute on shore, he went to sea, where he \nwas under no obligation to pay his men, who paid \nthemselves at the expense of their enemies. He \nput to sea with twenty galleys, and, shortly after \nleaving Algiers, he met with a galley from the \nLevant, from which he received information that \na powerful armada was preparing in Constantinople \nfor an expedition against the Christians. He \n‘steered for Coron in the Morea, where he was \nalmost immediately joined by the Ottoman fleet, \nthe commander of which force was overjoyed to",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.112019",
          "source_file": "india.history.resource.112019_112019_djvu.txt",
