      {
        "finding_id": "section-4f30ad9cb13b61377faa",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "white coloniser, Captain Cook, as a pirate. By portraying the pretence \nof empire as an act of piracy, Boyd's image neatly aligns with our aim in \nthis book to unsettle the conventional oppositions between piracy and \nsovereignty, toppling the hostis humani generis from its pillar of infamy. \nPursuing this aim raises difficult questions about the concept of piracy and \nits definition in relation to global history. Is piracy an essentially European \nconcept that is applied, often inappropriately, to world historical contexts in \nthe wake of European overseas expansion from the late fifteenth century? \nWhat, in different historical and cultural contexts, sets piracy apart from \npurportedly legitimate uses of maritime violence, such as warfare at sea, \nreprisal, protection, and privateering? Is there a principal difference between \npiracy or armed robbery at sea and similar acts of plunder and violence on \nland? What are the meanings and connotations of the concept of piracy \nin different linguistic and cultural contexts? These are some of the key \nquestions that this book sets out to explore. It does so by looking at the role \nthat piracy played in different cross-cultural contexts during the period \nof European overseas expansion and imperialism from around 1500 until \naround 1900.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
