      {
        "finding_id": "section-bb1cd894010e6a74a97b",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Empires and states during Antiquity projected themselves as the antithesis \nof pirates and the guarantors of maritime security. A major aim of the \nRoman Empire in the wake of the Punic Wars was to suppress piracy and \nuphold maritime security in the Roman Mare nostrum. Failure to do so could \nthreaten the very foundations ofthe Roman Empire. When the Cilician sea \nraiders disrupted maritime traffic in the eastern Mediterranean during the \nLate Roman Republic, the statesman and general Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus \n(Pompey) led a campaign in 67 BCE that supposedly cleared them from the \nsea in just three months. Speaking in the Senate in favour of Pompey and \ndefending the decision to grant him extraordinary military powers, the \norator and statesman Marcus Tullius Cicero (106-43 BCE) represented the \nsituation as one ofunprecedented crisis that threatened the very existence \nof the Roman Republic.” In the view of Cicero, pirates were the antithesis \nofthe state and of civilized society. Cicero was the first Roman Latin author \nto use the word pirata, which he borrowed from the Greek, although he also \nused other terms, particularly praedo (robber, thief), to refer to the Cilicians \nand other sea robbers.”°",
