        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In all of these respects — the notion of pirates as the enemies of all, the \nthreat posed by pirates to state and society, and the usefulness of pirates for \nthe extension or accumulation of power — Roman notions of piracy were to \nreverberate in the history of Europe and, from the late fifteenth century, the \nworld. From this time, the texts of Cicero and other classical authorities were \nrediscovered in Europe. De officiis, in particular, was one ofthe most read \nand secular books in Europe during the Renaissance, and it was translated \nto several vernacular European languages from the late fifteenth century?! \nIn this way, Cicero's writings on piracy and other topics came to exercise a \ngreat influence on the development during the Early Modern period, which \neventually would lead to the establishment of international law and the law \nof the sea. Together with other salient features of the pirate image, such \nas the association with opulence and drunkenness, many elements of the \nEuropean perception of the pirate thus had their origin in Roman times. \nThey gained renewed currency as Europeans began to extend maritime \ntrade and colonization in the fifteenth century?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
