        "verified": false
      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-dfa9ac85d034c71dc6fd",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "This Hobbesian situation on the world's oceans made it necessary to \ndraw a line between legal and illegal maritime violence and to establish a \nlegally enforceable distinction between pirates and privateers. On paper, \nthe distinction may have seemed straightforward enough: pirates were \nessentially bandits at sea who operated without the permission or sanction \nof a recognized sovereign, whereas privateers held a commission from a \nrecognized sovereign in the form a letter of marque and confined their raids \nto attacking enemy ships in wartimeJ? In practice, however, the policy of \nsanctioning privateers, which remained common on the part of European \ngovernments throughout the Early Modern era, created a vast grey zone \nbetween legal and illegal maritime violence. The practice thus gave rise \nto a number of problems and anomalies in the international maritime \nsphere. First, one nation's pirates were another nation's privateers or even \njust innocent navigators. The different perspectives gave rise to very dif- \nferent interpretations, for example in the case of the six Dutchmen who",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
