      {
        "finding_id": "section-1c3b42cc276c4313c4d6",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In view ofthe obviously chaotic and unregulated situation on the world's \noceans, European jurists in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries strove \nto formulate a workable legal definition of piracy. The Italian jurist Alberico \nGentili held that it was the sovereign who had the right to define who was a \npirate, and as this doctrine gained currency throughout Europe, it provided \nthe sovereign with an instrument of great power that extended far beyond \nhis or her shores.*° It also afforded European governments the right to \ndefine non-European actors as pirates simply by withholding recognition \nof sovereignty or statehood, as discussed by Ostlund and Buchan in the \ncontext of the so-called Barbary states in the Mediterranean. This points \nto a third problem concerning the distinction between legal and illegal \nmaritime violence, namely, the question of who was a sovereign. The answer \nwas not always clear even in Europe, as demonstrated in the chapters by \nBuchan and Kempe, and the problem was even more pertinent in relation \nto non-European rulers. Were raiding fleets sponsored by North African or \nSoutheast Asian rulers, for example, to be considered piratical or as part of \nthe naval forces of the rulers in question?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
