      {
        "finding_id": "section-4ac3283731b936bcb48b",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "From an Asian point of view, European commercial and imperial expansion, \nboth before and after the turn ofthe eighteenth century, looks very much \nlike a massive wave of organized piracy, neatly captured by Peter Earle's \nphrase “piratical imperialism.” This observation, however, raises the ques- \ntion of what terms were used in non-European languages to denote what \nEuropeans called piracy. Were there established terms in the vernacular \nAsian and African languages or were European terms, such as piracy, corsair, \nand privateer, borrowed and introduced in these languages as a result of \nthe interaction with European navigators? Patricia Risso's article showed \nthat there were both indigenous and borrowed words for illicit maritime \nviolence in Arabic and Persian, and the contributions of the present book \nextend and corroborate this conclusion. Although it is sometimes claimed \nthat there was no indigenous Malay term for piracy, at least not before the \nmid-nineteenth century, illicit maritime violence was conceptualized much \nearlier.9 Throughout the Malay Archipelago, a wide range of ethnonyms \nwere used to refer to piratical activities or maritime raiding, as discussed \nby Jennifer Gaynor and Hans Hágerdal in their contributions. Sometimes, \nthese ethnonyms became generic terms for piracy or maritime raiding, \nsuch as the Malay word lanun, derived from Illanun (or Iranun), an ethnic \ngroup originating from the southern Philippines and which, from around \n1770, acquired a formidable reputation for maritime violence and coastal \nraiding throughout Southeast Asia.^*",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
