      {
        "finding_id": "section-0ebe076a6f2af32ad9bf",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The history of piracy in this period charts a complicated terrain of \nsubtle meanings and mendacious matters of state. The suppression of \npiracy was used in England, for example, as a way of implementing claims \nto sea power. Yet, those same pirates were executed precisely by those \nauthorities and institutions that had formerly promoted them. It was \ncomparatively easy for sea captains to obtain letters of marque or other \nlicences to take booty at sea, so long as it was taken from the authorizing \nsovereign's enemies. The widespread practice of international privateering \ncreated a class of nationally unattached privateers who might obtain \nlegitimate employment from different sovereigns. The activities of these \ninternational troublemakers inevitably led to an agreement between \nthe European nations to set limits to the privateering system. They did \nso by extending the concept of piracy. Whereas the question at issue \nat the beginning of the seventeenth century was how to establish the \nmost extensive right as possible to take booty at sea, as formulated by, for \nexample, Hugo Grotius,’ from the second half of the seventeenth century, \nEuropean sea powers sought to regulate this right of booty more strictly. \nThe price paid for this change in international prize and privateering \npolicy was that the not inconsiderable reservoir of battle-hardened sea \nrobbers it created began to operate independently. This ultimately led \nto the phenomenon of a globalized European piracy that connected the \nAtlantic with the Pacific and the Indian Ocean in the so called “Golden \nAge of Piracy\" at the beginning ofthe eighteenth century.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 82666,
          "end_char": 84317,
          "start_line": 1631,
