      {
        "finding_id": "section-a5e0dc70a5cadc6776ee",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It was only towards the end ofthe sixteenth century, however, that piracy \nwas first conceptualized in the context of international law. In 1588/89, the \nItalian lawyer Alberico Gentili took up Cicero's definition of the pirate in \nhis De iure belli libri tres, explicitly linking the hitherto unspecific general \nconcept of an enemy with international law.'? According to Gentili, war on \npirates is just because they have violated the commune ius gentium: \"Piratica \nest contra ius gentium, & contra humanae societatis communione.\"5 The \npirate violates the laws of war as a part of international law and therefore \ncannot enjoy its protection. Gentili agrees with the legal tradition that a \nwar against pirates can neither be officially declared, nor concluded by a \ntreaty, and can only end with the death of the pirates or their victory. The \nItalian lawyer went beyond previous tradition in seeing the pirate — even \nmore so than the robber in general — as the general enemy of humanity \npar excellence.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 88385,
          "end_char": 89395,
          "start_line": 1729,
