      {
        "finding_id": "section-dee0b2388b7b22e279c6",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Around the mid-seventeenth century, a systematically elaborated concept \nof piracy was available in legal theory, which ensured that sea robbers could \nbelegally treated as universal enemies. But what was the situation in legal \npractice? To whom was this concept applied in international contexts? How \ndid one become such an enemy of all peoples and nations in the second \nhalf of the seventeenth century? The above-mentioned George Cusack was \nexplicitly reckoned among the humani generis hostes, the “Publique Enemies \nto Mankind,” when he was brought before court as a pirate after his arrest \nin 1674. Cusack’s career as a privateer and a pirate is not untypical and yet \nalso unusual at the time. It is not untypical because many elements can be \nfound in his biography that are characteristic of contemporary privateering \nand piracy. Similar biographies can be found in archives on pirate trials \nsuch as those documented for the London Admiralty Court.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1674
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
