      {
        "finding_id": "section-e2410dfe0472e080a282",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The anonymous author of The Grand Pyrate excoriated Cusack. In the \naccount of this short text he becomes the prototype of a hostis humani \ngeneris. The seriousness of his crimes was unmistakably denounced. The \nauthor underlined not only that the victims of his plundering raids were \nof many different nations — English French, Dutch, Danes, and Swedes — \nbut also that he and his accomplices were particularly perfidious, as they \ndisguised themselves as ordinary sailors in order to join and then seize ships \nand crews. Cusack was depicted as a notorious serial offender with a high \nlevel of criminal energy, whose privateering voyages extended over many \nyears and covered a wide radius of action - Africa, the West Indies, the \nAtlantic, the North Sea - and whose readiness to use violence was always \nhigh. For example, in Barbados, when his disguise was revealed, he had not \nhesitated to open fire immediately on a warship. It appeared particularly \nunscrupulous that he had set the crews ofthe captured ships adrift in small \nboats or left them behind in unseaworthy ships, which meant their almost \ncertain death. It was also emphasized that Cusack had explicitly declared \nhis intention to wage battle against (almost) the entire world. He was said \nto have called upon his accomplices after the capture of the Hopewell to \nswear in writing that they would seize or sink ships and vessels of all nations \napart from England.?? The description of the universal hostility of Cusack’s \npirates culminated in the recital of a drinking song that the deep-sea devils \nsupposedly sang daily, intoxicated by alcohol and their own evil:",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 111584,
          "end_char": 113225,
          "start_line": 2164,
