      {
        "finding_id": "section-d4d3515648f1b0088d99",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the West Indies, Cusack's activities led to tension with Portugal at the \nhighest political level. When the French crew who had been imprisoned by \nthe Spanish after the seizure of the São José heard, after their release, that \ntheir ship had landed near Boston they informed the owner in Lisbon, who \nthen demanded compensation from the government in New England. The \ncomplaint was coolly rejected by the Boston authorities, who pointed out that \nthe time limit of one year for appeal had elapsed. The case then even came up \nbefore the Crown in London, as the Portuguese owner officially demanded \nthat the English King should order the Boston Governor to compensate for \nhis losses.9? Furthermore, Cusack's activities had also fuelled the flames \nofthe conflict between the English Crown and its American colonies. The \nsupport for Cusack in Montserrat and Boston painfully reminded the London \ngovernment that the English in the overseas colonies, who were bound by \nthe Navigation Acts of 1651 to restrict the import and export trade of the \ncolonies to the mother country, were willing to cooperate with pirates \nand smugglers in order to circumvent the monopoly and to acquire highly \ndesired raw materials and commercial goods.5?",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1651
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
