      {
        "finding_id": "section-ca9d89eaa83b426e6bab",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Gradually, the dark side of internationally practised privateering became \nmore visible. Unscrupulous sea robbers like Cusack were so dangerous \nprecisely because they always found the support of some nation or other \nand were never the enemies of all nations at once. The phenomenon also \nenabled a degree of deniability. Responsibility for a privateer such as Cusack, \noperating under one sovereign's letter of marque, could be shrugged aside \nwhen found to be the subject of another sovereign. Cusack's sea robbers \nwere ultimately condemned by the system that had produced them in the \nfirst place. Almost all of them had, like Cusack, served the English Crown \nin the wars against the United Provinces, either as soldiers or mercenaries, \nin the navy or as privateers. The end of hostilities with the Netherlands \nleft them unemployed. Most of the men - 25 years old on average - had \nlearned nothing but seafaring and the trade of war. Their know-how had \nprobably protected most of them from the execution of their sentences, \nas Jenkins and the Admiralty judges were content to make an example \nof Cusack and his “right-hand man,” Harker. One can easily imagine how \nmany of the reprieved were later active again as privateers in the service \nof His Majesty, just as Cusack had been officially engaged as a privateer \noperating against Dutch ships off the coast of Scotland, despite his former \nactivities as a Caribbean pirate. From privateer to pirate and back again: \nthe enemies of the international community were also products of the \ninternational system as such.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
