      {
        "finding_id": "section-40eaa24f5a9ffc8f0bbb",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The General History was published to capitalize on the currency of public fear \nand fascination in piracy, at a time when Britain was waging a campaign to \nexterminate pirates from distant seas. The book presented piratical exploits \nin the Caribbean, across the Atlantic, and into the Indian Ocean and Red \nSea. The British public's interest in the pirates committing such far-flung \ncrimes had been fed by a range of sensational trials, widely reported in \nperiodicals and further publicized in plays and broadsheets.\" One of the \nmost notorious of these cases involved the Englishman, Henry Avery or \nEvery (1659—?). He had caused an international scandal by making himself \nfabulously rich in 1695 with a raid on the Ganj-i-Sawai, a ship belonging to \nthe Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb, returning wealthy and well-connected \npilgrims from Mecca across the Red Sea. Though Avery and his crew tried \nto lie low by establishing their own pirate community on Madagascar, the \nBritish attempted to assuage the Mughal Emperor's outrage by staging a public \ntrial in 1696. While Avery managed to disappear without trace, members of \nhis crew had been captured, were found guilty, and executed.? The trial of \nAvery's crew (followed soon after by the trial and execution of William Kidd \nin 1701 for other acts of piracy committed in the Indian Ocean) reinforced \nan evident public interest in piracies on seas far from Britain. The idea took \nhold in the public imagination of Avery as a “successful pirate,” and a host of",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1659,
          1695,
          1696
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
