      {
        "finding_id": "section-965da9a355b980040f04",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Queen Ann’s Revenge. As others have pointed out, pirates would stop at \nnothing to legitimize their violence, even claiming to fight on behalf of a \ndeposed king.'? Yet, what is interesting about this feature of the General \nHistory, in light of Mist’s glowing review of it, is that it highlights a wider \nambivalence surrounding the figure of the “pyrate” as hostis humani generis \nin British legal and political thought in the so-called Golden Age of Atlantic \npiracy.\" The “pyrates,” as presented here, were no doubt volatile and violent, \nbut they were also possessed of a unique kind of virtue. If this deliberately \nambivalent portrayal of the “pyrates” was a product of Mist's Jacobitism, \nit is likely to have originated in his opposition to the English Whigs who \nousted King James II from Britain in 1688, and their use of the charge of \n“piracy” to consolidate a new sovereign on the throne.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1688
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
