      {
        "finding_id": "section-a524a513a74cebd3d958",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In recent years, political theorists have argued that “Golden Age\" pirates \nachieved a kind of social contract.” Pirate \"Articles\" were agreements made \nby men who inhabited “a genuine state of nature,” and were “created to exit \nthe Hobbesian Jungle.\"? A variety of scholars have drawn attention to the \nself-organization among pirate crews as forms of resistance to sovereignty \nand the sanctity of property in Britain, Europe, and in their various colonies \nand empires.”° Some scholars have gone further by identifying pirates as \nrational actors who contracted for limited political authority within their \ncrews and communities; in effect, exemplifying in their pirate \"Articles\" the \nsocial contract long imagined by political theorists, (most notably John Locke",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
