      {
        "finding_id": "section-86d3471559c360f6c83a",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "then legitimately rebel and replace their government.’ Far from sounding a \nclarion call for revolution, however, Locke was at pains to argue that this was \na conditional right to rebel that could be invoked only when the government \nhad actually become tyrannous. To deny such a right, Locke argued, would \nbe to argue that \"honest\" subjects “may not oppose Robbers or Pirates.\"5 \nLocke's final, if tangential reference to piracy in the Two Treatises elaborated \nthis point in reference to the time worn \"ship of state\" metaphor. In Locke's \nhands, however, the metaphor was given an unusual twist by likening the \nsubject ofa state drifting towards tyranny to a passenger aboard a \"Ship [...] \ncarrying him, and the rest of the Company to Algiers.\"? The significance of \nthis reference could not have been lost on contemporaries, for whom the port \ncity of Algiers on the North African coast was a well-known resort of Barbary \ncorsairs.*° These raiders, both African Muslims and Europeans, operated \nfrom the north coast of Africa, attacking European shipping and coastal \ncommunities.*' Of most importance in contextualizing Locke's reference \nwas that chief among the corsairs' objectives was to take captives for sale \ninto slavery to work on galleys or as domestic servants, estimated to have \nnumbered in the thousands from Britain alone in the early decades of the \nseventeenth century.*? Hence the significance of Locke's analogy. Passively \nwatching the drift of a state towards tyranny was akin to the position of \ncaptives knowing that their ship was destined for Algiers, and they for \nslavery. Both captive and subject were bound for intolerable slavery at the \nhands of a power no more legitimate than that of a mere pirate. Moreover, \nand this was the burden of Locke's metaphor, neither captive, nor subject \nshould be deceived that the temporary setbacks or illusory concessions made \nalong the path to tyranny had any greater bearing on the final destination \nthan the crosswinds that occasionally beset the corsair's galley.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 170661,
          "end_char": 172706,
          "start_line": 3282,
