          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Atthis point, itis worth asking what Locke might have known ofthe pirates \nwho populated his text. Although his earliest references to piracy in the \n1660s indicated no particular knowledge, by the time he came to write \nand then continue to revise his Two Treatises, Locke had ample access \nto a variety of sources on piracy — both printed and personal.49 Locke \namassed a considerable personal library, among which his collection of \nethnographic writings produced from Europe's expanding global and impe- \nrial connections has been estimated as one of the finest.*” This ethnographic \ninterest provided one possible avenue through which Locke may have been \nexposed to first-hand accounts of the politics of piratical communities in the \nCaribbean as he published and then revised his Two Treatises throughout \nthe 1690s. Although published in 1689-1690 (some ten years after it was \noriginally written), Locke was unhappy with this first edition, and so he \ncontinued to refine the work. Further editions appeared in 1694 and 1698, \nand he left instructions with his executors for a further edition after his",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1690,
          1694,
          1698
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
