      {
        "finding_id": "section-b29493d2eff6136ec637",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "When Locke wrote of government by consent his thought was not animated \nby democratic aspirations so much as by the overriding concern to limit \nthem, by balancing a right to rebellion against tyranny with the need for \nsecurity of property. This concern took shape in the campaign of England's \nWhiggish elite, for whom Locke worked, to secure a Protestant and pliable \nsuccessor to the throne to follow the Catholic James II. The Two Treatises \nwere originally conceived and composed, but never published, in the context \nof the “Exclusion Crisis\" of 1679-1681. At that time, Locke's patron, the \nEarl of Shaftesbury, led an unsuccessful Parliamentary and propaganda \ncampaign to have a Bill passed to have King Charles II's Catholic brother \nand heir, James, excluded from succession to the throne. Although Locke \nselectively and circumspectly circulated his manuscript at this time, he did \nnot publish it. Over succeeding years, he continued to refine the manuscript \nand eventually published it anonymously in the wake of the “Glorious \nRevolution\" of1688-1690 that had succeeded in deposing the then king, James \nII, and replacing him with his Protestant daughter Mary and her husband \nWilliam, Prince of Orange. The publication of Locke's text in 1689-1690 \napparently provided a justification for this rebellion and deposition, even \nthough it originated in an earlier crisis.9? The text trod a fine line between \nrevolution and security, as Locke himself acknowledged in attempting to \nconstrue the right to rebellion as a limited and last resort. The Whiggish elite \nhad learned the lesson from Britain's earlier Civil War and Commonwealth \ngovernment (1642-1660) that the common people desired and would fight \nfor their own liberty and democratic rights that they conceived to be more \nexpansive than the property-owning elite were willing to allow. For this \nelite, there was much to lose in opening the door to more radical claims. \nThe Glorious Revolution was their attempt to secure the state that would \nsecure them and their property, and by so doing protect and project Britain's \ncolonial and maritime commerce.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1642,
          1660,
          1679,
          1681,
          1689,
          1690
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 184763,
          "end_char": 186896,
          "start_line": 3541,
          "end_line": 3569,
          "printed_page": null,
          "scan_indexes_0_based": [],
          "pdf_file": null,
          "pdf_reference_base": "LANE_ROOT",
          "original_inspected": false
        },
        "reported_cited_pages": [],
