        "verified": false
      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-aad36790845449c8c463",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It is therefore significant that at the very time Locke was working on \nthe second edition of the Two Treatises, piracy presented one of the first \nserious legal challenges to the new regime he and his patron had worked \nto establish. King James II's ill-fated attempt to win his throne back by \nforce of arms in Ireland ended in 1691 with a capitulation to the victors \nthat allowed him to withdraw his troops and supplies to France. By the \nTreaty of Limerick, he and his army were accorded the honour of defeated",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1691
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 186899,
          "end_char": 187414,
          "start_line": 3571,
          "end_line": 3577,
          "printed_page": null,
          "scan_indexes_0_based": [],
          "pdf_file": null,
          "pdf_reference_base": "LANE_ROOT",
          "original_inspected": false
        },
        "reported_cited_pages": [],
        "decision": "REVIEW_CONTEXT",
        "reason": "THEMATIC_SOURCE_PASSAGE_NOT_VERIFIED_BASE",
        "extraction_method": "DETERMINISTIC_THEMATIC_PASSAGE_READING",
        "uncertainties": [
          "ORIGINAL_NOT_INSPECTED",
          "SOURCE_GENEALOGY_PENDING",
          "INDEPENDENCE_UNRESOLVED",
          "TOPONYM_AND_USAGE_REQUIRE_SEMANTIC_EXTRACTION",
          "REGIONAL_SCOPE_REQUIRES_REVIEW"
        ],
        "evidence_state": "ACQUIRED",
        "original_inspected": false,
        "verified": false
      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-8a2e8bb47df11f3dbe7e",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "As John Bromley has demonstrated, the capture and trial of a handful \nof these captains in 1692-1693 took place in a hostile atmosphere fed by \nfevered computations that their raids cost British trade as much as £3 mil- \nlion.9* In November 1692, the Lords of the Privy Council and the Lords of \nthe Admiralty convened to try captains John Golding, Thomas Jones, John \nRyan, Darby Collins, Richard Shivers, Patrick Quidley, John Slaughter, and \nConstantine de Hartley as pirates. The Advocate of the Fleet, Dr William \nOldys (or Oldish) was briefed to prosecute the captains as pirates, which he \nrefused on the grounds that these defendants were not the “common enemies \nto all mankind\" but “privateers” acting under the “colourable authority \nremaining in King James.”®5 At issue between Oldys and the Lords was not \nthe practice of privateering itself, but the question of a “colourable authority \nremaining in King James\" to issue such warrants following his deposition \nfrom the British throne in 1688. Oldys's contention was supported by Sir \nThomas Pinfold, who addressed the Lords on the identity of defendants as \n“pirates.” As Pinfold saw it, “a Pirate was hostis humani generis,\" meaning that \nany person so described must be a literal enemy to all humankind. Pinfold \nclaimed that the defendants “were not Enemies to all Mankind” but simple \nsailors prosecuting war by means of entirely legitimate privateering (duly \nwarranted by a sovereign monarch), \"therefore they can not be Pirates.\"56 \nSuch a literal defence elicited smiles from the justices, one of whom was",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1688,
          1692,
          1693
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
