      {
        "finding_id": "section-3366564f16a4b0829d94",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The argument was taken sufficiently seriously by the Lords of the Ad- \nmiralty that Oldys was summarily dismissed and replaced by the Deputy \nJudge Advocate, Matthew Tindal. He had no scruples about trying the men \nas pirates. His prosecutorial arguments in 1692 resulted in the conviction of \nthe officers as pirates, some of whom were hung, and their bodies displayed \nin gibbets between low and high tide marks on the Thames as a warning to \nothers. In 1694, Tindal amplified his arguments in a publication that was \nto become an influential work on piracy in international law: An Essay \nConcerning the Laws of Nations and the Rights of Sovereigns. Tindal argued \nthat James had no claim to be regarded as a sovereign, having abdicated his \nthrone and its prerogatives, and thus he could not issue valid privateering \ncommissions.5* The ubiquitous Latin tag used to name the figure of the pirate \nin law, hostis humani generis, was, Tindal wrote, “[...] neither a Definition\" \nor a \"Description [...] but a Rhetorical Invective to shew the Odiousness of \nthat Crime.\"69",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1692,
          1694
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 190566,
          "end_char": 191639,
          "start_line": 3639,
