      {
        "finding_id": "section-e2281161d880caca57fa",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "What kind ofa crime was it? For Tindal, the crime of piracy imbibed two \norders of wrongdoing. First, for a subject who \"receives Protection from a \nGovernment, and has sworn to be true to it, yet acts against it,\" piracy isa \ntreasonous betrayal.” Second, in repudiating allegiance and obligation to one \ngovernment, the pirate subverts all governments by holding in contempt the \n\"Ties and Bonds that unite People in Civil Society under any Government.\" \nHence, the pirate is an \"Enemy to all Governments.\" As Tindal saw it, the \n\"Certainties\" on which the law of nations was founded mandated freedom \nofthe seas and security of commerce, requiring the assertion of sovereignty \nover and above the spurious claims made by an *unkinged\" monarch who \nhad now “dwindled” to become not just a private person, but a mere “pirate” \nwho no longer possessed the sovereign right to declare war and peace.” In \nthis formulation, Tindal appeared to echo Locke's association ofthe figures \nof the tyrant and the pirate. What lay at issue, as Tindal made clear, was \nnot simply the prosecution of a handful of hostes humani generis, but the \nde-legitimation of a one-time sovereign who now represented to the new",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
