      {
        "finding_id": "section-29c3dc3b5b021a48255c",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "During the nineteenth century, colonial debates about piracy in maritime \nSoutheast Asia encompassed views that ranged from taking piracy asa \nsign of state dissolution, much as it appears in contemporary failed states \ntheory, to functionalist explanations of piracy as simply inherent to how \nsome states worked. The latter view, in which maritime marauding was \nseen as a practice common to Southeast Asian statecraft, typified the much \nearlier remarks Tomé Pires made about coastal polities in sixteenth-century \nSoutheast Asia. Pires, a Portuguese apothecary who spent time in Malacca \nshortly after the Portuguese conquest in 1511, left detailed notes about the \nmain trade items of ports throughout Southeast Asia and beyond it, as part \nof his effort to plot their commercial ties with Malacca. He also recorded the \nnaval capacity of port cities throughout the maritime world of the Indian \nOcean and Asia, from the Red Sea to Japan. Although Pires specified little \nabout the interactions between mariners and their nautical leaders, he took \npains to note how many boats and rowers lay at the disposal of different \nports and rulers.?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
