      {
        "finding_id": "section-e35b8fe2fac4ecd6c891",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "What makes piracy international, then, is not so much its occurrence on \nthe high seas, but instead its juridical location at the limits of sovereignty. \nIndeed, most historical piracy has taken place not on the high seas, but \ninstead, in the offing, the visible offshore area beyond inshore navigational \nhazards. Rather than focus on piracy as an activity in “non-state” spaces, or \nby “non-state” people, actions that might be considered piracy in particular \nhistorical circumstances are best understood in relation to the relevant \narray of political authorities and law. Piracy as a phenomenon only occurred \nin international arenas where legal spaces between polities stretched and \nsometimes overlapped. These interpolity spaces, and the diplomatic, legal, \nand naval resources brought to them by different players, structured both \nthe character of claims about piracy, as well as effective denials of piracy. \nThrough such claims, authorities gathered political and legal legitimacy to",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
