      {
        "finding_id": "section-7a8e4090994f82e2fcc7",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Second, the piece probed the capture of a Portuguese carrack by three \nDutch ships under Jakob van Heemskerk in early seventeenth-century \nSoutheast Asia, along with the European political and legal context of this \nevent. For Grotius, defending this ship's seizure near the Malacca Straits, \npirates, whoever they might be, could never be the kind of admiral who he \nmade every effort to portray as identified with the nascent Dutch state. \nEven though Heemskerk lacked letters of marque and was forbidden from \nengaging in conflict, Grotius portrayed him as an extension ofthe state—a \nstate Grotius presumed already to exist—arguing that Heemskerk was \nboth its representative and agent. The Mare Liberum cannot reasonably be \nunderstood separately from this context, as it comprises one ofthe chapters \nin Grotius' larger argument, bolstering the nascent Dutch state and this \nwould-be (or perhaps would-not-be) pirate's supposed inseparability from \nthat state. Third, the article took up the question of piracy in connection \nwith nineteenth-century views on the proliferation of raiding in Southeast \nAsia and intercolonial attempts to curb it.?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
