      {
        "finding_id": "section-be0bffbe39527f50ccfc",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In fact, the lens of the state is not the only optic available. Historical \nanalyses of piracy that rely on the writings and actions of self-proclaimed \npirates provide one avenue of analysis with, as it were, built-in critiques of \nthe state, though it is not easy to come by such material. While not nearly \nas abundant as materials written by court scribes, politicians, jurists, and \nofficials, records left by pirates provide a privileged point of view that, with \nample contextualization, allow historians to reap unique insights into the \nmotivations of pirates and the dynamics that contributed to their actions. \nMarcus Rediker, whose work exemplifies this bottom-up approach in the \nsocial history of piracy, drew on the actions and words of pirates to argue \nthat they made not another, if alternate, state in the eighteenth-century \nAtlantic, but rather formed a multicultural, democratic, and egalitarian \nsociety — albeit one often beset by violence.'?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
