      {
        "finding_id": "section-f9504c43ae018e271847",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "My own modest attempt to derive theoretical points from the analysis of \nsuch shared dynamics began with the metaphor of “the offing”: the visible \nwaters beyond inshore navigational dangers. With its between-land-and- \nsea location and its perspectival lability — from which side is one viewing \nthese waters? — the offing provided a neat model for understanding the \nstructure and dynamics shared among situations that involved the question \nof piracy. As I clarify below, \"the offing\" implies a spatial deixis. Moreover, \nin settings where a question of piracy hangs in the balance, the metaphor of \nthe offing provides a tool for thinking about deixis at the level of socio-legal \ninteractions. In other words, the offing helps one understand piracy as a \nform of socio-legal deixis.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
