      {
        "finding_id": "section-4ac0bcb1d666b0fa99d6",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Only after explaining these interrelated structures of perception, drawing \nout the metaphor of the offing, and applying it to historical cases focused \non the question of piracy did the offing’s resemblance to the concept of \ndeixis become apparent, which necessitated an explanation of its theoretical \nrelevance. Borrowed from the Greek adjective, deiktikos, meaning “pointing,” \nor “indicating,” deixis is typically understood as the process of “pointing” \nvia language to the extra-linguistic context. Deixis belongs to the subfield \nof linguistics known as pragmatics, which studies how people understand \nand produce speech acts in particular, concrete situations. Deixis belongs to \npragmatics because it directly involves the relationship between the struc- \nture of language and the context in which it is used.\"® Deictic expressions \nuse “pointing” language (for instance, “here,” \"there\") that situates a speaker \nin relation to a shared context. Spatial or place deixis is only one form of \ndeixis. Languages that express familiarity in pronouns, or use honorifics to \nindicate higher status, engage forms of person deixis. Although I had never",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
