      {
        "finding_id": "section-67156a7717d2e66261ac",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Tiworo, the so-called pirates' nest, was a polity in its own right, not an \noutsider beyond states, and one could not call its mariners non-state actors. \nOn the contrary, Tiworo maintained alliances, first with the state of Makas- \nsar, and then with the latter's rival, Boné. Curiously, such disparagement - as \nCornelis Speelman demonstrated - of Southeast Asian mariners as piratical, \nhad not been characteristic ofthe Dutch work published in 1663 about the \nGreat Ambon War, which related the 1655 attack on Tiworo.” Nor did such \ncharacterizations as piratical, or designations of piracy, appear in archived \nletters between VOC commanders and ship captains engaged in fighting \nmariners from Tiworo and elsewhere during these conflicts ofthe 1650s and \n1660s. Tagging Tiworo as a nasty pirates' nest only took place in retrospect,",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1655
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
