      {
        "finding_id": "section-f4456959827ed50fa857",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In his Piracy and Politics in the Malay World (1963), Nicholas Tarling argued \nthat the eighteenth-century expansion of European trade into “Malay” areas \nthreatened the economic foundations of indigenous coastal states, weaken- \ning the sultanates and allowing for the development of piracy. However, \nmany have cast doubt on Tarling's analysis. Anne Lindsey Reber traced this \nanalysis to Raffles' evolving views on Southeast Asia. Often omitted or cited \nonly in passing by many authors, though extensively quoted and extolled \nby James Warren, Reber's 1966 thesis examined nineteenth-century British \nwritings on Malay piracy as a historiographical problem.3° She demonstrated \nhow early nineteenth-century British colonials saw \"piracy\" in the western \nStraits, which reached across the archipelago from Sulu bases, as sufficient \njustification for intervention and conquest. Raffles, she explained, intro- \nduced biases into the historical record, for he, especially, came to write \nabout nineteenth-century designations of piracy in Southeast Asia in ways \nthat tied it to the notion of native state decay. Raffles largely faulted the \nDutch for this situation of supposed native state decay, an unsurprising \nascription of blame given early nineteenth-century intercolonial wars over \nterritorial control. Raffles proposed to ameliorate these conditions through \nnew commercial centres under British domination.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
