      {
        "finding_id": "section-93764aae3547d868e6a1",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Ina rather similar fashion, \"failed states\" made quite a splash in the press \nwhen piracy around the horn of Africa soared in the 1990s. However, it \nshould be noted, in contrast, that one did not hear such talk of failed states \nto explain the high incidence of piracy in the contemporaneous Malacca \nStraits. When piracy in the Malacca Straits later declined, though, countries \nengaged in international cooperation were nevertheless quick to credit \nand congratulate themselves, having learned that cooperation was vital to \ntheir aims, much as intercolonial cooperation effectively brought “piracy” \nto an end, for a time, in the late colonial period. Curiously, those kudos of \ncontainment ignored how fishing communities in the Straits continued to be \ntargeted. At the same time, one finds it encouraging that Somalia observers \nnow more commonly integrate the political economy of fishing into analyses \nof piracy off its coast, where fish stocks were being raided by vessels from \nelsewhere.33 Fishing is serious business. In the 1970s, Indonesia's small-scale \nfishers took matters into their own hands with Molotov cocktails when \ntrawlers damaged their gear, and sometimes their persons, along with the \nlarge scale of extraction that threatened their livelihood. A nationwide ban \non trawlers in 1980 was only partly successful.?^ In recent years, Indonesia's \nMinister of Marine Affairs and Fisheries has become famous for her orders \nto blow up foreign fishing vessels in Indonesia's waters.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
