      {
        "finding_id": "section-6eb292d8a9eca0781c46",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "While Raffles' and Tarling's interpretation of piracy's origin looked to \nnative state decay, their explanation, which assigned agency to colonial \nactors, focused more on the consequences of commercial disruption. Theo- \nries of failed states, more than Tarling's or Raffles’ explanations of native \nstate decay, often imagine a lack of policing resources as one of piracy's \nmain causes, which simultaneously positions \"state failure\" as a pretext \nfor external intervention. Critics of the failed state concept have done a",
