        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The image of piracy has largely been shaped by a few early European descrip- \ntions, such as Exquemelin's History ofthe Buccaneers of America (1678) and \nJohnson's A General History ofthe Pyrates (1724), which oscillated between \nthe romantic and the abhorrent. These pirates are placed outside of the \nnorms of society, entering an internalized system of modes of behaviour, \nhowever violent and turbulent. While less publicized, seaborne raiding in \nSoutheast Asia has also been emblematic in the form of “Malay pirates,” \nfor example via Salgari's novels about Sandokan. Such literary references, \nand the fact that the Malays were primarily confronted by British ships \nand therefore found their way into works in English, tends to overshadow",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1678
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
