      {
        "finding_id": "section-3e9b2c57eba425cd7318",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "the early seventeenth century (perhaps only partly in the case of Lombok).'? \nThe six Muslim petty kingdoms of Sumbawa were formally brought under \nthe dependency of the VOC after 1669, while Lombok was left outside the \nDutch orbit and soon became a bone of contention between the Hindu \nBalinese and the West Sumbawans.? The other case is the seascape around \nTimor, further to the east, a small-scale or even stateless and low-technology \narea that partly came under nominal VOC suzerainty between the 1613 \nand 1653, and partly under Portuguese domination in the same period.’ \nHere, I follow the occurrence of maritime raiding after 1669 to the late \neighteenth century. For the purpose of this chapter, I focus on seaborne \nrobbery beyond the prerogatives of land-based polities. This approximates \nthe traditional European understanding of \"piracy,\" and was understood as \nsuch by European observers (in Dutch reports, zeerovers, etc.), although, as \nmentioned, it is not exactly paralleled by indigenous terms. The approximate \nMakassarese terms are (tau-) belo and serang, while robber in general is \ngorra, bango, or lanong.'5 Some of these appear to derive from raiding \nmaritime peoples (Tobelo, Ceram, Ilanun), which indicates a propensity \nto associate outsiders with violent crime and highlights the ambiguities in \nfinding a conceptual correspondence. Geographically, I compare an area \nwith intense food production and Hindu-Javanese and Islamic cultural \ninfluences, with a dry and relatively resource-scarce area, characterized \nby small-scale and genealogically defined communities mostly practising \nancestral religions. What range of activities by the Bugis-Makassar seafarers \ncan be traced in the material, and how did forms of cooperation alternate \nwith outright “piracy”?",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1613,
          1653,
          1669
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
