      {
        "finding_id": "section-9b9e834eddf4a0251f4e",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The main protagonists here were two Makassarese princes ofthe blood, \nKaraeng Pamolikang (d. 1704) and Karaeng Jarannika (d. 1700). We meet \nKaraeng Jarannika on various occasions in the 1660s and 1670s, as one of \nthe more prominent chiefs of the King of Gowa, and a person who drew \nsuspicion in the eyes of the VOC as being an unreliable element. In 1674, \nhe was involved in a scheme with two other princes to attack Bima in East \nSumbawa with their seaborne retainers. The reason was allegedly a matter \nof honour: the Sultan of Bima had supposedly ordered the digging up and \nburning of the corpse of the King of Tallo' (the junior “twin kingdom\" of \nMakassar) who had died on Sumbawa the year before. To the outsider, this \nlooks very much like a loose pretext for legitimizing acts of piracy, but similar \nmotives are found in other contexts among Makassarese aristocrats and refer \nto the traditional virtues of siri, dignity, and pesse, communal empathy.'?",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1674,
          1700
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
