      {
        "finding_id": "section-4edca9a584c218d54082",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "From this point onwards, the royal raiders ran out of luck, in part because \nof the notorious untrustworthiness of their chiefs. The defeated princes \nwithdrew over the strait to Palaba in Lombok where the Balinese King of \nKarangasem received them: “this was no wonder since they were then all \nunited and loyally assisted each other.\" However, the Byzantine intrigue \nthat pervaded \"Indonesian\" politics at the time soon made the position of \nthe pirates even more vulnerable. The Sumbawan rulers suggested to the \nBalinese king that he would do well to exterminate the rascals (die schelmen \nmoest uytroeyen) in order to ingratiate himself with the VOC. The king \ndecided to act quickly to deal with the troublesome guests. He invited the \npirates to a feast with the spectacle of *mirror-fighting Balinese” — perhaps \nthe well-known Baris dance where performers appear in rows with lances in \ntheir hands. At a given sign, the Balinese turned on the hapless Makassarese \nand impaled each one with two or three lances, an operation so swift that \n“not even a cat or dog could have escaped.” Jarannika lost his life along with \n151 retainers, while the sly Pamolikang had wisely remained in the pirate \nden and was able to set sail and sneak away in time.”9",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
