        "verified": false
      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-2fe102c424f3c44f1258",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The persistent but ultimately unsuccessful enterprise of the Makassarese \npirate princes warrants a few interesting observations. The porous line \nbetween state-condoned warfare and sheer piracy is striking. Fighting on \nbehalf of the Dutch and its allies could immediately be followed by blatantly \nanti-VOC activities. Rapid changes of alliances made for clashes with a \nnumber of polities of any religion or ethnicity. In spite of the independent \nacts of the two princes, their ties to the VOC vassals in Gowa and Tallo’ were \nnever entirely broken. In the highly hierarchical system of Bugis-Makassar \nsociety, their aristocratic “white” blood carried with it an awe that combined \nwith their apparent martial prowess. This can also be seen in the ambigu- \nous stance of their Sumbawan victims; at one moment they would marry \ninto local royalty and act as protectors, in the next they would ravage the \ncoasts of the erstwhile allies. Their Muslim identity may have played a \nrole in machinations against the Dutch and Balinese, but in both cases \nenmity alternated with alliance in a somewhat confusing way that seems \nto transcend religious borders. To the extent that we can trace the concrete \naims of their acts — the material is usually Dutch with all its bias — they \ntried to secure bases from whence to build up a position of political power, \nsuch as West Sumbawa, Selaparang, and Manggarai. This is indicated by \nthe open or clandestine alliances that shifted with great rapidity. In that \nway, they might classify as political entrepreneurs rather than pirates of \nthe classical outlaw type. As pointed out by Leonard Andaya, Makassarese \npost-1669 migrations to other parts of Indonesia, such as Banten, Madura, \nJambi, and Palembang, led to shifting alliances with local rulers where the \nMakassarese leaders took great care to guard their princely prerogatives \nin spite of being threadbare refugees.?5 On the other hand, the self-willed \nand untrustworthy (and thus piratical) pattern of behaviour eventually \nbecame self-defeating.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1669
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 298307,
          "end_char": 300361,
          "start_line": 5658,
          "end_line": 5685,
