      {
        "finding_id": "section-3f343349c1ad2d733c82",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Makassarese seafarers are frequently mentioned after 1669 in the Dutch \nrecords from Kupang, the hub of VOC power in the Timor Islands. Their \nactivities were part of a larger overall movement where they travelled \neastwards, via the Tomini Gulf or Southeast Sulawesi, and effectively \ncircumvented Dutch bases, especially gaining economic leverage in the \neighteenth century.?? Since the sources relatively seldom speak of Bugis, \none suspects that the term Makassarese alludes, without distinction, to \nanyone coming from South Sulawesi. From the Dutch horizon, they usually \nact as troublemakers, being either *smugglers\" who bring goods without \nVOC permits, or outright pirates. There are contrary indications that the sea \nmigrants actually got on relatively well with the Portuguese, who anyway \ndid not have the VOC's means to control trade prerogatives.?? The fleets \nof ships appearing around the Timorese coasts were initially relatively \nsmall although they later became more substantial. In fact, they often \nseem to lack strategy; or rather, they adopt a strategy of flexibility, seeking \nopportunities for trade or robberies as they found them in the vulnerable \nsocieties of eastern Indonesia. In 1671, for example, it was reported that a \nsingle Makassarese ship had abducted 12-13 people in a coastal settlement",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1669,
          1671
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
