      {
        "finding_id": "section-f70dec10aa765415ee06",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In seventeenth-century Europe, a common notion of a pirate (occur- \nring in the most archetypical form in the West Indies) was a sea thief, an \nenemy of the human species. In a way, the pirate was not even an enemy \nproper, since pirates had no “commonwealth,” no court, no treasury, no \nconcord of citizens; rather, he was a freebooter outside of any law.59 Here, \nagain, the framework of concurrent concepts of piracy becomes useful. The \nVOC officials might have had such notions in mind when describing the \ntroublemakers who passed review before their eyes, judging from invectives \nsuch as zeerovers (sea robbers, pirates), rovers (brigands), schelmen (rascals). \nAgainst this, it apparently weighed lightly when the court of Gowa, address- \ning the Dutch authorities, referred to the slain Karaeng Jarannika as een \nvoornaam Macassarees princekint (a noble Makassarese princeling).5\" Nor \ndid the Dutch know or care that the tribes of distant Timor kept stories of \nMakassarese, who brought the secret of iron to the island, or intermarried \nwith the highest aristocracy, aside from their more violent approaches. In \nfact, the two types of Makassarese pirates were involved in a net of cultural \naffinities, migratory patterns, and economic exchange that did not entirely \nplace them outside human “commonwealth.”",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
