      {
        "finding_id": "section-16830452b16a638918b9",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "work to emerge on piracy in the Indian Ocean? where the argument has \nbeen that the colonial state endorsed some forms of violence as legitimate \nand others as not. Such a perspective on piracy as a phenomenon that \nwas both discursively constructed as well as a real practice in response \nto myriad forms of political pressure, including colonial violence has had \nearlier incarnations in older nationalist Indian historiography that critiqued \nEuropean assumptions about the Indian pirate. In any case, the Indian \npirate did not command the same imagination or claim the narrative of \nadventure or freedom or privateering, and occupied a rather narrow and \nconstricted space, literally hugging the littoral avoiding the high seas. \nAs Hagerdal notes with respect to Bugis-Makassar in this volume, piracy \nalong the western Indian coast was littoral piracy that targeted coastal \ntrade rather than shipping on the high seas. This meant that the limits of \nterritorial expansion were more apparent in the littoral and coincided with \nthe early colonial state attempting to simultaneously reformat the power \nstructure in the seas by marking off the coastlines more sharply than ever.",
