      {
        "finding_id": "section-cacfbeca112a6032e778",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "As other contributors to this volume have noted, the history of piracy has \nbeen characterized by concurrent concepts and understandings of the \nphenomenon. Yet, there is also an historiographic concurrence. Early studies \non piracy in the Indian Ocean tended to extol the strength and resourceful- \nness of the European imperial navies in subjugating the lawless pirates of \nthe Indian Ocean, both European and Asian. Even here the Indian pirate \nwas not held on a par with the European, who, in most cases, was seen as \na privateer working for the interest ofa specific European power while the \nIndian pirate was, at best, a petty criminal. For example, John Biddulph, in \nhis classic account ofthe pirates of Malabar, referred to European pirates as:",
