      {
        "finding_id": "section-fc05b5362c9278a9869f",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The validity of such representations, the politics behind such a construc- \ntion lies at the heart of my project. Equally, it seeks to investigate the shifts \nin the working of littoral politics that engaged maritime mercenaries in their \npolitical calculus. In undertaking such a study of reality and representation \nof piracy and predation, the study works under the assumption that the \nadvent of the Europeans in the Indian Ocean following the blazing guns \nof Gama and his merry men introduced very substantive changes in the \nway the ocean was understood as a site of commercial activity and political \npower. Following the work done by scholars such as M.N. Pearson, Genevieve \nBouchon, and Jean Aubin among others, I argue that, notwithstanding earlier \npractices of deploying violence as a political resource by several states in \nthe Indian Ocean, the articulation of the cartaz-cafila-armada system by \nthe Portuguese was more comprehensively coercive and inductive and had \nprofound consequences. It forced Indian traders who had always worked \nin a mare librum to accept passes, pay for them, and call at designated \nports of call to pay customs, thereby adding to their operating costs. The \nsystem was not especially popular and in regions such as Malabar that \nwitnessed large-scale violence, anti-Portuguese coalitions were formed \nby local rulers and dispossessed coastal groups, seen by the Portuguese as \npirates! Subsequent work (Elliott, Layton, Subramanian) has demonstrated \nhow piracy was a label that Europeans used to describe any resistance to \ntheir politics of ordering the seas and their exclusive claims to policing this",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
