      {
        "finding_id": "section-dfaee1dad41029da045c",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The edict went on to insist the necessity of protection of merchants and \nfishermen (kolis), the latter being the backbone of the naval force and spoke \non modes of naval action at sea during war and conflict, and of resisting \nthe claims of European merchants who did not behave like merchants. \nThus, what seems apparent from a close reading of the document is that \nEuropeans had to be effectively countered at sea and, for this, defending \nterritorial waters was crucial. The most efficient way of ensuring this was \nto farm out responsibility to armed mercenaries and sea captains who \nrode the seas.’ Superficially, the sea captains or coastal chiefs resembled \nEuropean privateers lending their expertise at sea to mount limited naval \ncampaigns but the resemblance ended there. Many of them actively cul- \ntivated interests around aquatic resources — rights to wrecks and fishing, \nfor instance, became part of coastal politics. From the latter decades of \nthe seventeenth century, the emergence of sea captains who fought for the \nMaratha state and for smaller local chiefs was striking, as their operations \nand skirmishes at sea replicated the wars on land. The emergence of small \ncoastal powers — the Malvans, Kudals, or Desais of Sawantvadi - testified to \nthe growing parcellization of littoral authority with a growing assertiveness \nover territorial waters. None of these chiefs saw themselves as pirates — they \nwere fighting men whose exploits were in the service of states. The greatest \nof such privateers who assumed a bigger role was Kanhoji Angria (1669-1729) \nbut he was dismissed by the English Company as a lawless pirate.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1669
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
