      {
        "finding_id": "section-978e5a26d24d9e594eb0",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "included local bosses, merchants, and even temple trustees in Dwarka, \nwhere a particular form of piracy and piratical politics prevailed. Thus, \neven if we can see the Okha case of piracy working within the interstices of \nsovereign authority and markets that the English power wished to define, \nwe can also adopt another lens for understanding the phenomenon. This is \nto ground piracy as an integral part of littoral political arrangements that \nembodied the tension between caste Rajputs and seafaring coastal groups, \nand how this was held in balance by a set of quasi-political and financial \narrangements in which local merchants, markets, and the temple at Dwarka \nplayed an important mediating role. This requires a careful reading of the \narchive along the grain and its multiple registers, and listening closely to \nthe murmurs of merchants, the complaints of captured pirates and the \noutrage of their bosses, and the latter's conceptions of what they thought \nof as custom and practice.\"",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
