      {
        "finding_id": "section-1a6d257133b8c6a2d789",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The subsequent narratives of piracy played up some of these elements. In \ntracking the history of piracy's representation in Gujarat, two moments seem \nespecially important. The first is that of Colonel Tod, who represented an \nimportant voice of colonial ethnography that came long after pacification, \nand the other of nationalist folklore specialists like Jhaverchand Meghani.?? \nFor James Tod, pursuing the idealized feudal ruler, it was convenient to \npress local stories of heroism and valour into a grand narrative of romantic \nRajput feudal honour, while for Meghani it was important to imbue the \nstory of the outlaws with a degree of agency. Both drew and worked from a \nrepertoire of tales and memory that carried vestiges of maritime activity, \nincluding piracy that was an integral part of local economies and political \narrangements. In both cases, the phenomenon of piracy, although deployed \nasanimportant political resource, was detached from the idea of sovereignty \nnotwithstanding some ofthe convergences between Indian and European \npolitical arrangements at sea. In both cases, the idea of piracy was always \nnested within a local and community structure of customs and obligations \nand thus emptied out of all political traction.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
