      {
        "finding_id": "section-3a6a21c52fb78b00353f",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "But, writing just before his death in 1600, Mustafa Ali observed that \nover the past generation it had become increasingly difficult to distinguish \nbetween the small-time pirates then following similar career paths along the \nAdriatic and Aegean coasts and the North Africa-bound corsairs they may \nhave aspired to become.’ The reorientation of Ottoman naval resources \nand a series of profound political, economic, environmental, and military \nchallenges contributed to an explosion of piratical violence in the eastern \nhalf of the Mediterranean in the decades following the Ottoman conquest \nof Cyprus and the Ottoman defeat at Lepanto, both in 1571, and the Ottoman \ncapture ofthe Spanish-held fort of La Goletta at Tunis in 1574, which marked \nthe end of over half a century of naval conflict over the North African \nlittoral and led to a formal truce with Spain that was ratified in 1581. As \nOttoman naval defence efforts foundered in this era of endless land wars \nand fiscal crisis, a diverse assortment of Catholic corsairs and English and \nDutch merchant-pirates poured into the Ottoman Mediterranean, while \nhomegrown Muslim and Christian coastal raiders proliferated from the \nAegean to the Adriatic. Ottoman-aligned corsairs based in North Africa \nand along the southern Adriatic and Ionian coasts also took advantage of \nthe chaos, raiding the Ottoman subjects and shores they were otherwise \nexpected to protect.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1600
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
