        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Indeed, korsanlik in the seventeenth century was sometimes deployed in \ncircumstances free ofthe religious and political baggage scholars normally \nassociate with the term. For instance, in 1617, when a Greek Christian ship \ncaptain accused another Greek Christian ship captain in an Ottoman court \nof firing his cannon at his ship, driving him overboard, and stealing his \ncargo of wheat — piracy in its purest form - the scribe quoted the plaintiff \nas saying that the defendant had, in that instance, “done korsanlık.” The \nblurry semantic distinctions between the various forms and practitioners of \nmaritime violence, and their accompanying shades of legality, are exempli- \nfied by the many meanings of the word levend.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1617
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
