          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "A word of uncertain origin, sometimes translated as “adventurer” or \n“young man,” levend could denote officially recognized Ottoman corsairs, \nindependent freebooters with no ties to the state, or naval auxiliaries more \ngenerally.? A ship, its captain, and the fighting men on board could all be \ncalled levend. Although the word was usually used for Muslims, it carried \nno specific ethnic or geographic connotation in Ottoman usage and was \nemployed both in the core Ottoman lands and in North Africa. For example, \naccording to Antonio de Sosa, in late sixteenth-century Algiers, all \"soldiers \nof the sea - whether renegades, janissaries who go privateering [...] or Turks \n[...] are commonly called /evends.\"? The word was used for auxiliary or \nirregular forces on land as well, and by the second half of the sixteenth \ncentury, levend had also acquired the pejorative meaning of \"bandit.\"? In \nshort, both Ottoman and foreign (Christian) maritime raiders, including",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
