      {
        "finding_id": "section-c0e3d183beb40187e883",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In Ottoman administrative documents, official views of such actors \nand their methods were clarified somewhat by context and through the \nuse of various modifiers. For instance, the somewhat redundant gónüllü \nlevend korsanları (literally “volunteer levend corsairs”) and similar such \nconstructions could be used to describe corsairs or volunteer irregulars in \nimperial employ and stationed in an Ottoman port.\" On the other end of \nthe legal spectrum, harami levendleri paired the word for robber or thief \nwith levend to indicate pirates, as opposed to loyal irregulars. Harami was \nalso used, either by itself or as an adjective, to describe a ship or a captain, \nto mean pirate in maritime contexts. Another common combination, levend \neskiyalart (levend rebels, outlaws, or bandits), could be used to describe both \npirates at sea and highwaymen on land; it could also denote auxiliaries \ngone rogue. Banditry, like piracy, was viewed as a crime against the state \nand thus bandits and rebels were virtually synonymous in Ottoman usage. \nSuch people were also referred to as ehl-i fesad, villains or evildoers - liter- \nally, “people of corruption\" — which spoke to the insidious impact of their \nillegal activities on the proper order of society and their exclusion from its \nranks. So, for example, Kara Hamza, Captain Osman, “Gypsy” Manika, and \nthe sixty men who manned their galliots for raids on merchant ships and \nAnatolian coastal villages in the vicinity of Mytilene in 1588 were described \nsimply as ehl-i fesad when the Sublime Porte ordered that they and their \nships be captured and that they be sent directly to Istanbul for exemplary \npunishment.”",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
