          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Sometimes, Ottoman scribes employed only descriptions of the types of \nship or their sailors, such as harbi kafir kalyonlart (enemy infidel galleons) \nor kaytk levendleri (levends with small skiffs, ie. coastal raiders); frigateer \n(firkateci), viz. the sailor of a frigate, was an especially common byword for \npirate, particularly those that preyed on Ottoman subjects.” In short, piracy \nwas defined situationally rather than occupationally, while corsairing was",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
