      {
        "finding_id": "section-825289edc2a9cfb7e748",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The fact of the matter was that the same factors that had made naval \nirregulars valuable to the Ottomans made regulating them incredibly dif- \nficult, and this was not just true of those operating out of semi-independent \nNorth Africa. The Sublime Porte had relied to some degree on levends for \nmaritime security and intelligence gathering since the fifteenth century, but \nits reliance on irregulars increased dramatically during and immediately \nafter the 1570-1573 conflict with Venice and its Holy League allies. The \ndefeat and near-total destruction of the Ottoman fleet at the Battle of \nLepanto in October 1571 deprived the Ottoman government not only of \nhundreds ofships, but ofthousands of experienced seamen, oarsmen, and \nsoldiers.?9? As a result, Ottoman administrators turned to the levends of \nthe Adriatic and North Africa who had missed or survived the debacle at \nLepanto to fill the security gap while the navy worked to recruit fresh men \nand rebuild. In the disorder and fog of war, there were myriad opportunities \nfor corsairs tasked with pacifying the Aegean islands to engage in illegal \nslaving raids, and for ambitious amphibious bandits along the Adriatic, \nIonian, and Aegean coasts to grow their gangs and expand their range? \nThe end of the war in 1573 brought little peace to the Ottoman Mediter- \nranean as erstwhile corsairs persisted in piracy, and the situation only got \nworse as rampant inflation, successive wars against Safavid Iran (1578-1590, \n1603-1618, 1623-1639) and Habsburg Austria (1593-1606), the disastrous \nCelali revolts (1595-1609), and dynastic crisis vied for the attention and \nresources of Ottoman administrators between the 1570s and 1640s?! The \ntrade-offs facing Ottoman administrators relying on irregulars for maritime",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1603,
          1606,
          1609,
          1618,
          1623,
          1639
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
