      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-135f199e26d642d0970d",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the 1670s, Evliya Gelebi observed that all of Aya Mavra's \"frigateer \nlevends\" wore red fezzes and red vests with patterned silk sashes around \ntheir waists, just like the Algerine corsairs who sometimes cruised in Ion- \nian and Adriatic waters and occasionally participated in joint ventures \nwith the local levends. Evliya encountered the same styles being worn \nby the Albanian levends of the port towns of Durrés and Vloré, located \njust to the north of the narrow entrance to the Adriatic. These levends \nwere natives, unlike the motley crews on Lefkada, but they too provided \nmaritime security and conducted reconnaissance missions and they too \nengaged in frequent piratical attacks (often carefully planned in advance) \nagainst nearby Ottoman, Venetian, and Ragusan targets.3? By donning the \ncostume of Algiers, the sometime-rebel levends of the Adriatic and Ionian \ncoasts masqueraded as holy warrior corsairs, even when an inconvenient \npeace rendered them pirates.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
