        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It was an unfortunate fact that the levends of Aya Mavra did “not have \ngood relations with the people of other places,” as Evliya put it, “but they are \nbrave and courageous and talented soldiers and they are a thorn in the eyes of \nFrengistan (the land ofthe Franks, viz. Christian Europe).\"' And that, at least, \nargued for a policy of responding loudly to serious incidents — calling for the \narrest ofthe \"rebels\" and the destruction of their craft — but otherwise avoiding \nsustained enforcement efforts that might exacerbate the situation. Thus, even \nthough the Ottoman governor ofthe Morea dispatched a force to Aya Mavra \nin 1675 to burn the levends’ ships, their piratical attacks persisted until 1684, \nwhen the Venetians joined the new Holy League formed in the aftermath of \nthe failed Ottoman siege of Vienna the previous year, declared war on the \nOttoman Empire, and made the conquest of Lefkada their first priority.*?",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1675,
          1684
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
