          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "But just as the levends of Lefkada dressed like the corsairs of Algiers, \nthereby associating themselves with the largest and most respected inde- \npendent corsairing outfit in the Muslim Mediterranean, most maritime \nraiders looked for ways to legitimize their activities, to position their \nraids within the traditional practices and conflicts of the major corsairing \norganizations or to defend them with a variety of religious and secular \njustifications. As we have seen, when holy war no longer applied, self-defence, \nretaliation, and reprisal provided the most plausible explanations. The \nback-and-forth raiding of the Adriatic-Ionian frontier was often couched in \nsuch language, and Algiers and Tunis alike employed similar excuses when \nIstanbul demanded explanations for raids on Venetians. In the aftermath \nof corsair attacks on the Venetian Ionian islands in 1624, for example, the \nTunisian leadership explained to the Ottoman and Venetian envoys sent \nto secure the release of the captives that its corsairs had no choice but to",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1624
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
