      {
        "finding_id": "section-f4890cf9da14ac4bc882",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Consider the example of the title character in the late seventeenth-century \nStory ofthe Jailor-Captain Mahmud and His Victories over the Damned Hell- \nDwelling Maltese. Set in the 1670s, this Ottoman Turkish tale tells of a French \ncorsair galleon whose jailor enlists his ship's Muslim captives in a mutiny. \nAfter they seize control of the ship, the jailor, now captain, explains to his \nmixed crew of Ottoman Muslims and French Christians that *we must join \nand take the flag of some power or an ocak [lit. “hearth,” the term used for \nthe governments in North Africa]; it is not reasonable for us to continue alone \nlike this.\"^ There is no question in this instance that the erstwhile Catholic \ncorsairing vessel will continue in its former profession, but it requires a \nnew sponsor, since independent piracy is not considered a viable option. \nThey run through the possibilities: the Ottomans, they decide, would arrest \nthem as pirates and imprison them; Algiers is too greedy and might well \nsteal their ship; Tripoli is too poor and has little to offer them; and so they \nsettle on Tunis, which is sufficiently wealthy, powerful, and welcoming. \nEventually, like so many other North Africa-based European renegades, \nthe French jailor adopts Islam and the name Mahmud, and battles Maltese",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
