      {
        "finding_id": "section-9181dd4f810c191d7d89",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "man administrators called “people of corruption,” “rebels,” and “thieves.” \nNevertheless, disavowed Ottoman, English, and Dutch privateers all found \nrefuge in North Africa, where they received the authorization they sought \nto continue their war under new banners. And yet, a significant number \nof those active in the early modern Ottoman Mediterranean whom we \nmight call pirates were not engaged in predatory raiding full time — they \nwere coastal guards, merchants, or fishermen who did so whenever it was \nconvenient, profitable, and relatively safe. As we have seen, from the Aegean \namphibious bandit to the Algiers-bound corsair, all such people could \nsimultaneously fall into one or more of the categories of levend or korsan, \nbandit or rebel, holy warrior or thief, frigateer or evildoer. The diversity of \nthe practitioners and expressions of maritime violence and the ambivalence \nofthe authorities towards them are reflected in the diverse and frequently \nambiguous Ottoman Turkish vocabulary employed to describe them. And \nso, when surveying the landscape of maritime violence in the sixteenth- and \nseventeenth-century Mediterranean, the right question may not be who \nor what was a pirate, but rather, at what point did raiding become piracy?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
