      {
        "finding_id": "section-3c5ca5d201846200cfb7",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "topics in Chinese history, few if any scholars would place piracy at centre \nstage. Indeed, no China scholar has examined the relationship that piracy \nhad with empire building and the legal regime upon which the state rested. \nImportant studies by Janice Thomson, Anne Pérotin-Dumon, Eliga Gould, \nLauren Benton, Michael Kempe, and others, although adding greatly to our \nunderstandings about the role that piracy has played in the operations of \nempire and law, nevertheless are Eurocentric in that they focus on Western \nimperialism and say little about how non-Western imperiums and legal \nregimes developed or functioned. In this chapter, I shift attention to the \nconstruction and internal dynamics of the Qing Empire, sovereignty, and \npiracy between the seventeenth and early twentieth centuries. Put simply, \nthis chapter takes a China-centred perspective.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
