      {
        "finding_id": "section-7c773a909595ba3dccfb",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "What see developing in China's late imperial age (roughly seventeenth \nto twentieth century) were three overlapping and competing legal regimes: \nfirst, that ofthe Qing imperium whose laws and jurisdictions stretched little \nbeyond the shoreline; second, that of the European empires (particularly \nPortugal, the Netherlands, and Britain), which sought to impose their own \nuniversalistic laws and jurisdictions over all oceans; and third, that of the \npirates themselves, who, as outlaws, were left to devise their own codes of",
