        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "I divide this study into two main sections. In the first section I discuss \none oftraditional China's fundamental geopolitical conventions: the binary \nconcept of inner and outer oceans. Traditionally, China conceptualized the \nwater world as two vague spheres of inner and outer oceans, which had \nimportant implications on how imperial China ordered its laws and wars \nagainst pirates. In the second section, which is divided into three periods of \npiratical upsurges — early Qing (1630s—1680s), mid-Qing (1770s—1810s), and \nlate Qing (1840s—1910s), I focus more specifically on episodes of piracy and \nthe problems of sovereignty in the late imperial period.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
