          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "as a domestic problem. In Europe, legal authority became associated with \nthe extension of sovereignty on the high seas. In fact, after 1673, at least \nin Britain, all cases of piracy had to be tried in admiralty courts, whose \njurisdictions were restricted to the high seas, rather than to home or coastal \nwaters. Sovereignty, in other words, followed the ship, so that mariners fell \nunder royal jurisdiction even when far away from home.^ Notions of law \nand sovereignty in imperial China, however, did not extend much beyond \nthe littoral, at least not until the late nineteenth century. Piracy, especially \nlarge-scale piracy, posed threats to the imperium’s internal security, domestic \nsovereignty, and ability to maintain law and order inside the realm, not on \nthe high seas.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1673
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
