      {
        "finding_id": "section-2bc0550caae7895db7e2",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Seen fundamentally as an internal problem, what Europeans called \npiracy was considered a form of banditry in China. In the Qing, the primary \nanti-pirate law came under the statute on *mounted bandits\" (mazei) of \nManchuria. Qing law made clear, strict distinctions between leaders and \nfollowers in meting out punishments. Convicted pirate leaders received the \nharshest penalties afforded by the law: normally decapitation and exposure \nof the head or, in the most serious cases, death-by-slicing. Furthermore, \nsince these criminals were considered guilty of committing such grievous \noffenses they were routinely executed right after trial in accordance with \na special procedure known as “summary execution by royal mandate\" \n(wangming xianxing zhengfa). This was an extraordinary legal procedure \nin that it allowed high-ranking provincial and military officials to side-step \nregular judicial procedures so as to expedite executions, without awaiting \nthe required approval of the emperor. Convicted followers, however, were \ngenerally sentenced to exile as military slaves.» As China had no overseas \ncolonies, unlike European governments that transported convicted pirates to \npenal colonies in remote areas ofthe globe, Chinese governments sentenced",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
