      {
        "finding_id": "section-7226c11a18c986f4c41c",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The outer ocean, in a cosmographic sense, was the realm of pirates.? It \nwas a boundless, nebulous, and unregulated space where pirates gathered \nand sought to maximize their autonomy and power.? As the author of the \nlate eighteenth century edition of the maritime atlas Qisheng yanhai tu (A \ncoastal map of the seven provinces) duly noted: \"The sea off the [Chaoyang, \nGuangdong] coast at Qianyu, Jinghai, Che'ao, and elsewhere has the reputa- \ntion of being a pirate stronghold. In the morning, pirates assembled their \nships in the outer ocean, watching for the opportunity to plunder the coast \n[inner ocean] in the evening.\"? In the nearby coastal area of Lufeng in Map \n1, itis also significant to note that the area marked “pirate bay\" (zei'ao) was \nsituated in the vicinity ofa fort between inner and outer ocean spaces. Such \npirate bays were normally regular anchorages for pirate and fishing junks, \nwhich were not only indistinguishable from one another but also often the \nsame. China's outer ocean appears quite similar to Eliga Gould's description \nof the Atlantic's peripheral areas — referred to as a region \"beyond the line\" - \nas a violent, contested space with conflicting laws and sovereignties. It was \na place where people were unhindered to engage in all sorts of despicable \nactivities otherwise unacceptable back home on land.\"",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
