      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-f1fe9c94b637546f7a68",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "As far as the oceans were concerned, late imperial China’s naval strategy \n(since the late fifteenth century, at least) aimed at coastal defence and \nprotecting coastlines and hinterlands, rather than offensive campaigns \nbeyond the inner ocean. The problem of fighting pirates, however, was \nsystemic. Even in the best of times, the Qing military establishment was hard \npressed to combat piracy. Imperial naval forces were neither structurally nor \ntechnologically equipped to handle pirates, particularly the large-scale pirate \nleagues that appeared several times during the period under discussion \nhere. Military strategy was decisively land-centred, defensive, and highly \nlocalized. It consisted mainly of constructing and manning guard posts, \nbatteries, watch towers, and signal posts at intervals along the coastline, as \nwell as maintaining small flotillas of war junks for coastal patrols. In effect, \nthe forts and coastal patrols marked the limits of Qing sovereignty on the \nseas. The Qianlong Emperor made it clear that his navy was only responsible \nfor policing the areas of the inner ocean and that anything beyond that was",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
