      {
        "finding_id": "section-32bf95f073f05f39f0a1",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The most serious challenge to the Qing came from the Zheng clan - under \nthe consecutive leadership of Zheng Zhilong, Zheng Chenggong, and Zheng \nJing - which created a maritime empire and alternative state based first \nin coastal Fujian and later on the island of Taiwan. Seeing opportunity in \nthe political instability of the period, the Zhengs constructed their new \npolity based on a combination of trade, piracy, and political manipulation. \nIndicative ofthe strength of their piratical/insurgent forces both the Ming \nand Qing governments had to come to terms with these powerful leaders by \noffering them pardons and attempting to incorporate them into the imperial \nnavy. Unable to militarily defeat Zheng Zhilong, the Ming emperor in 1628 \nmade him a naval commander. After the Ming collapsed he surrendered to \nthe new Qing rulers, who quickly placed him under house arrest in Beijing, \nfinally executing him 1661. Many of his clansmen, including his son Zheng \nChenggong and grandson Zheng Jing, however, continued to resist the \nManchus under the banner of Ming loyalism. Between 1651 and 1683 the \nZheng clique oversaw a huge maritime empire whose core supporters came \nlargely from the ranks of pirates. After Zheng Chenggong and Zheng Jing \nhad refused to surrender, the Qing government made overtures to their \nsubordinate Shi Lang, who accepted a pardon and helped the dynasty turn \nthe tide against the Zheng regime on Taiwan, which fell in 1683. One year \nlater, Taiwan was annexed into the Qing Empire.'?",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1628,
          1651,
          1661,
          1683
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
