      {
        "finding_id": "section-460decfbfe18244e48a7",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The existence of such large groups of organized maritime raiders over such \nan extended period of time posed a serious threat to the Qing imperium's \nclaim to sovereignty. But they were not the only ones to do so. Foreign \nthreats also came from Dutch and Vietnamese agents, who in separate \nactions tried to co-opt Chinese pirates in resisting the Manchu takeover \nof China. Throughout this period, the Dutch vacillated back and forth \nbetween the Ming, Qing, and Zheng forces, always seeking their own best \nadvantage. At the same time that the Ming and Qing were trying to co-opt \npirates with rewards and titles, the Dutch on Taiwan also tried to convert \npirates to serving their cause in forcefully winning trade rights with China. \nClearly, all sides were interested in winning over pirates because they were \nformidable forces and serious threats to the political and economic stability \nof the whole region.?? The Sino-Vietnamese maritime frontier was also a \ncontested contact zone; for Qing officials the Gulf of Tonkin was a \"turbulent",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
