      {
        "finding_id": "section-06041e3c71b5b1ed2c46",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the mid-Qing (1770s-1810s), once again formidable groups of pirates \nconfronted the Qing Empire. Initially under the protection and support \nof the Tay Son rebel regime in Vietnam, a new, even larger wave of piracy \narose along the south China coast in the 1770s. The Tay Son Rebellion, which \nbegan in 1771 in the remote hill country of southern Vietnam, escalated \ninto one of the largest and bloodiest upheavals in Vietnam's history. As \nthe rebellion dragged on for over thirty years, the rebel leaders, in need of \nmoney and soldiers, turned to Chinese pirates, offering them safe harbours, \nweapons, ships, and a fair share of booty. Each spring and early summer, \navailing themselves of the southwest monsoons, Chinese pirates set off \nfrom their bases in northern Vietnam to plunder shipping and settlements \non the south China coast, and returned to their bases in the late autumn, \nwhere they were protected from Qing military retaliation. Cognizant ofthe \nissue of sovereignty, in 1796 the Jiaqing Emperor ordered his navy to pursue \npirates only as far as the border with Vietnam. Later, both sides agreed to \nmutually extradite captured pirates back to their respective countries for \ntrial. Most of the Chinese pirates faithfully supported the rebel cause right \nup to the Tay Son defeat in 1802.77",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
