      {
        "finding_id": "section-bafa2e0d11b1bd3091e0",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The late Qing era began with the Opium War in 1839 and ended with the \nRevolution of 1911. Now, the Qing Empire not only had to deal with a series \nof internal uprisings (Taiping, Nian, Muslim, and Boxer rebellions), but also \na much more aggressive foreign imperialism (both Western and Japanese) \nand foreign wars (with Britain, France, and Japan). Taking advantage ofthe \nchaos, new waves of piracy arose all along China's southern littoral with \nwell-organized gangs numbering in the hundreds and sometimes in the \nthousands. As in the past, Chinese pirates set up strongholds on offshore \nislands in the lower reaches of the Pearl River estuary, Gulf of Tonkin, and \nelsewhere in the outer ocean and thus outside the effective reach of the \nstate. Adding to the chaos, Western sailors and renegades also formed their \nown gangs or joined Chinese gangs.3°",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
