      {
        "finding_id": "section-4a163142de7b5f35876f",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Over the course of the late Qing period, the state's policies regarding the \ndual threats of piracy and foreign imperialism were continually evolving. \nIn dealing with the persistent problems of piracy at each stage the Qing \nimperium not only employed alternating - sometimes simultaneous — \nmilitary extermination campaigns and appeasement measures, but also \nenacted stringent administrative laws to regulate maritime trade and \npolicies to prevent pirates from receiving aid from people on shore (e.g. the \naojia mutual responsibility system for fishing and commercial junks). Even \nthough state and local strategies remained decisively defensive and aimed \nto handle pirates in coastal waters or once they came ashore, nonetheless \nat the same time — especially over the late nineteenth century - the Qing \ngovernment became increasingly concerned with intrusions of foreign \npowers in the outer ocean. For example, Dongsha (Pratas) Islands, which \nlay some 340 kilometres off the Guangdong coast and had for centuries been \nan anchorage for both Chinese fishermen and pirates, became embroiled in \ninternational controversy between China, Britain, and Japan, and was only \nsettled in 1909 with recognition of China's sovereignty over the tiny atoll.3",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 490987,
          "end_char": 492243,
          "start_line": 9278,
          "end_line": 9294,
