      {
        "finding_id": "section-ca37ca1c3fa2839aa2f3",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Chinese pirates, like their Western counterparts, formed \"escape societies\" \nto flee the coercion ofthe state and at the same time also engage the state in \nviolent predatory opposition3^ Though impermanent, pirate islands became \nautonomous outlaw communities — non-state spaces — beyond the reach of \nany polity. In their protected sanctuaries, pirates built shantytowns, settled \ntheir families, conducted trade, and made their own laws. As outlaws, they \nlived by their own rules and had no allegiance to any state. The existence \nof pirate communities on the coast seriously challenged Qing sovereignty \nalong its maritime frontier. Even more threatening were the pirate groups \nthat established strongholds inside river estuaries close to major urban \ncentres, such as Canton, Macao, and Hong Kong. Operating from their \nlairs pirates held hegemonic sway over coastal communities and shipping \nthrough tax bureaus and protection rackets. Pirates constructed a novel \nsocio-political identity for themselves, one that set them apart from and \nin contention with mainstream society and political institutions on shore.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
