      {
        "finding_id": "section-f89751a5dc5833261576",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "A cross-regional view shows how the Spanish fear of Chinese, Japanese, \nDutch, and *Moro\" maritime attacks triggered the construction of fortifica- \ntion and surveillance architecture. Even the built environment ofthe colonial \ncapital reflected the everyday fear of piracy: the sturdy fortification of Manila \ndated back to rumours spreading after threats of a Japanese invasion in the \n15905; previous encounters with Japanese pirates and the simultaneous \nprolific maritime violence in the China Seas turned vague rumours into \neffectful fear in Manila and real concern in Madrid and Mexico, from where \ndefence architecture was financed in this period. During the eighteenth \ncentury, the built environment of Luzon and Mindanao was complemented \nwith watchtowers and sanctuary stone churches for the protection of the \nlocal population against coastal raiding. Some of them serve as witnesses \nof this age of fear until today.'? However, at that point, neither Manila nor \nimperial centres in the Americas or Spain were able to assist financially. The \nfact that the majority of surveillance constructions and means of defence \nwere not financed by the colonial centre in Manila but grew out of local \ninitiatives and private donations tells us a lot about the social and political \nimplications of coastal raids’ accompanying discourse of permanent threat.”° \nThe fort of Zamboanga (first built in 1634) in Mindanao is a prominent \nlandmark reminding of Spanish counter measures against coastal raids.”",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1634
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
