      {
        "finding_id": "section-91e843f4227f42c6502e",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Two Spanish accounts, one by the Spanish soldier Miguel de Loarca \nand a later copy by missionary ethnographer Gaspar de San Agustín, \ndescribe the diplomatic dimensions arising from Limahong's assaults \non the fledgling Spanish colony in the Philippines.?? In the manner of \ninstrumentalizing foreign maritime threats for the sake of affirming \nSpanish military power both Spanish narrations memorialize Spanish \nsuccesses on the coastal battlefield. Moreover, all Spanish accounts are \nsuspiciously silent about the participation of the Chinese navy in fighting \nthe raiders.3? One should add that such Spanish descriptions ignored \nChinese participation in fighting organizations like the one controlled by \nLimahong, but also failed to identify the pirates as political actors within \nAsian trading networks 3?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
