      {
        "finding_id": "section-f418ec3132e224816915",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Inspired by an allegedly universally understood vocabulary, the incum- \nbent Philippines’ governor general Acuña used a piracy analogy in a letter \nto Tokugawa leyasu in June 1602. He described Dutch mariners in the China \nSeas as rebelling vassals of the king of Spain, compared them to pirates \nand boldly asked that the Dutch were sent to the Philippines where they \nwould receive just trial.5> Although similarities to a previous Japanese \nrequest to send all wako-pirates from the Philippines to Japan were obvious, \nIeyasu did not give in to Acufia's demands arguing that the Dutch were \nvery committed to him.59 Ieyasu, knowing the Dutch version of the story, \ncounted on potential future collaborations and soon equipped them with \nofficial Japanese trading licences: In 1604, captain Jacob Quaeckerhecq \nsailed on behalf of Tokugawa Hidetada to Patani, an act that marked the \nbeginning of lasting, albeit convoluted Dutch-Japanese relations for the \nfollowing 260 years.5?",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1602,
          1604
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
