      {
        "finding_id": "section-63867d603e9828fbdf26",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Soon after Dutch vessels first arrived in Southeast Asian waters in 1596, \nSpaniards feared Dutch privateering around the Philippines and Maluku. \nIndeed, in the year 1600, Olivier van Noort successfully crossed the Pacific \nand upon arriving in the Bay of Manila he made attempts to capture ves- \nsels coming and going from the Spanish port city. Bothered by what they \nidentified as acts of piracy, the Spanish took action against Van Noort and \nhis people. In a naval battle commanded by Antonio de Morga, they were \nable to capture one of Van Noort's two remaining ships, but lost their own \nflagship? At that time, the Spanish colonial administration cared less about \nthe threat of Dutch competition than about the loss ofthe Spanish ship and \nthe 120 people on board.® The image of the Dutch raiding Asian waters \ncirculated with Morga's bestseller Events in the Philippine Islands (Sucedos \nde las Islas Filipinas, first published in Mexico in 1609). Morga described in \ndetail the naval battle against the Dutch corsario Van Noort and how apt \nSpanish naval forces fended off enemy personnel before elaborating that \nthe Dutch corsario would have caused more harm had he been allowed to \nroam the seas.” Ever since, the book became an important reference for \nthe early Spanish history ofthe Philippines and thus shaped the reputation \nof the Dutch as pirates. Complementary to the Spanish interpretation a \npowerful visual source has left a strong imprint on popular and scholarly \ndiscourses: a son ofthe famous Frankfurt-based Southern Dutch illustrator",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1600,
          1609
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
