      {
        "finding_id": "section-e86edfdaed576b4b6ecb",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The article has argued, moreover, that any history of piracy is also a history \nof languages and labels operating in different power discourses. As such it too \neasily dismisses the veto of chronology. In pre-modern records, controversial \nlabels and their genealogies merged with ethno-centric biases and the burden \nof mercantile rivalries. In later centuries, such terms have developed new \nnotions and have become important instruments for imperial expansion, \nnation building and local identity politics. During the nineteenth century, \nwhen many of the treatises dealing with sixteenth- and seventeenth-century \npiracy in Philippine waters were drafted, these accounts got mixed up \nwith contradictions and notions of untapped possibilities of the Spanish \ncolonial state in Asia. Narratives of the historical Spanish presence in the \nEast were constructed discursively around the lack of security, leading to a \npeculiar self-awareness of a permanently besieged territory. A blend of these \ninterpretive layers has come to determine the memories of the people and \nthe interpretation of the historian. In the age of expansion and conquest, \nviolence determined all relationships between subjects and sovereigns.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 563481,
