      {
        "finding_id": "section-dafed69df2aebf39ec66",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The expansion of Sweden's seaborne trade to the Mediterranean had \nintensified from the middle of the seventeenth century, when Swedish \neconomic policies were formulated around new interests: the need for cheap \nsalt and the development of markets for Swedish staple commodities in \nsouthern Europe.” Rising salt prices in Setubal and Lisbon pushed Swedish \nmerchants into the Mediterranean, a region characterized by warfare and \nstruggle for control between the Habsburg Empire and Ottoman Empire. \nTensions between them simmered in coastal raids, semi-official privateering, \nand outright piracy. Throughout the latter seventeenth century, Constan- \ntinople's control over its North African vassals declined and the Barbary \nCoast became a base of operations for corsairs whose activities spread to \nMalta and Livorno in Italy. Swedish losses were considered so serious that \na first attempt to negotiate peace with the most powerful North African \nstate, Algiers, was proposed in 1667.5 An agreement never materialized \nhowever, in part because it was feared that a peace treaty between Sweden \nand Algiers might have caused irritation among other European powers. \nHence, by the time Thelaus came to write his dissertation in the early \neighteenth century, the problems of Barbary attacks on Swedish shipping, \nthe taking of ships and enslavement of crews, were so well-known in Sweden \nthat it was frequently reported in newspapers. Nationwide calls for alms \nwere made to collect ransoms, and the fear of pirates was a feature of prayer \nbooks and other religious writings, denouncing them as robbers without a \nland. Given its prominence, Thelaus's decision to discuss piracy might not \nseem surprising. Yet, De piratica needs to be understood in relation to its",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1667
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
