      {
        "finding_id": "section-8fdf6707a731ee3be83c",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "circles. In February 1699, Johannes Heldingh's dissertation Mauritaniam \nSeu Regna Fes: Maroccanum Et Algier ... [On the Mauritanian Kingdoms of \nFez, Morocco, and Algiers] provided a detailed description of the so-called \npirate nests along the North African coast. Johannes Reftelius returned to \nthe same question in his dissertation of October 1700, De pactis cum barbaris \n[On Treaties with Barbarians]. Reftelius considered the legitimacy of treaties \nwith non-Christian states, as Thelaus was later to do, but he stayed closer \nto questions framed by earlier scholastic theologians, namely: whether \nChristian nations could make pacts or treaties with so-called barbarians \nwho had no knowledge of, or disdained the Christian god??? Thelaus also \nframed his dissertation as a contribution to the naturallaw tradition of \nthought, but his argument was not so conventionally constrained.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1699,
          1700
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 594116,
          "end_char": 595008,
          "start_line": 11245,
          "end_line": 11256,
          "printed_page": null,
          "scan_indexes_0_based": [],
