      {
        "finding_id": "section-e08ba45d6f6c81d9c009",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "and centres such as Fez, Tunis, Algiers, and Tripoli, Thelaus noted that the \nBarbary corsairs were active throughout the Mediterranean and into the \nAtlantic. All of the corsairs' cities, except for Fez, were provinces paying \ntribute to “the powerful sultan” in Istanbul, but each of them retained \nconsiderable independence from his control. Thelaus wrote that the evident \ndangers along this coast, from Egypt to Gibraltar, were the reason why it was \nreferred to as Barbary, since the corsairs like \"ferocious harpies\" had infested \nthe coast, and *you would rightly not compare them with people but with \nlions, tigers, wolves and foxes.\" Thelaus's focus on the evil embodied by these \n\"corsairs\" was evidently narrow. They \"violate human laws when praying on \nEuropean shipping,\" he argued, seemingly indifferent to piracy committed \nagainst non-Europeans. Thelaus then provided a series of descriptions of \nBarbary cities and of their activities. Their distinguishing feature, he argued, \nwas thatthey mastered a trade in slaves taken from European ships captured \nat sea and even from raids on land. Thelaus described the Barbary slave \nsystem, its rules on the ownership of goods and captives and the nature of \ntheir servitude; he outlined the numbers of slaves held in Barbary, and the \narrangements in place for ransom. All ofthis was informed by references to \ncontemporary European authors, notably Johan Ludwig Gottfried (1584-1633) \nand Olfert Dapper (1636-1689).2° Both published important collations of \ninformation about the Ottoman states along the coast of northern Africa. \nWhathe took from these sources was an uncompromisingly harsh judgement \nof the corsairs' barbarity. Yet, Thelaus also drew out some ameliorating \nevidence, as he showed in discussing slavery:",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1633,
          1636,
          1689
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
