      {
        "finding_id": "section-d48479babdc3810cd6d4",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In the last chapter ofthe dissertation, Thelaus returned to the moral implica- \ntions ofthe problem of piracy. Thelaus's reasoning was once again based on \nthe stipulation that “natural reason\" and the “natural law\" was inscribed in \nevery human heart. These principles underlay his advocacy for “compassion” \nand “community among humans.” The problem was that people seldom \nfollowed the universal principle of “common sense,’ and therefore the “rule \nof the natural law has been wiped out by bad customs.” While bad customs \nsustained the evil of piracy, the slender hope was that bad customs could, \nover the course of time, be changed into better ones. It was on this basis \nthat Thelaus argued it might be possible for European states to negotiate \ntreaties with peoples he described as “barbarians and less civilized peoples” \nin order to oblige them into “friendship.” One may detest having to make \nsuch treaties, he reasoned, but by doing so they become instruments of \n“security,” and a weapon to force the recalcitrant to give up their piracy. \nHaving thus argued that pirates could be made amenable to natural \nlaw, the next important question was whether they might also fall within \nthe umbrella of international law? Thelaus here confronted head on the \nconventional definition of pirates as common enemies of humankind. Ifthis \ndefinition were sustained, pirates could have no recourse to international law \nbecause being “enemies to all, they are nota part of the human species.\" By \ninvoking the authority of Cicero, Grotius, and Pufendorf, Thelaus explained \nthat pirates could never constitute legitimate states or governments. Pirate \ngangs were established with the purpose of robbery, while governments were \nfounded with the intention to establish justice, to live honourably, and to \nrespect property. In other words, Thelaus accepted Grotius's reasoning that \npirates may not appeal to international law? Once again however, Thelaus \nwas prepared to leave the door slightly ajar. Though he accepted there was \na \"formal difference between [gangs of] looters and states,\" he nevertheless \nconceded that sometimes pirates did constitute a kind of power to rival states.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 611158,
          "end_char": 613351,
          "start_line": 11553,
          "end_line": 11581,
          "printed_page": null,
          "scan_indexes_0_based": [],
