      {
        "finding_id": "section-729a1b423df3f6e92e1b",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "They could at times marshal substantial numbers and some had proven \nthemselves able to command mighty military forces. What is more, he argued, \nin order to do that, those pirates maintained a kind of order that might just \nbarely be described as honourable. Echoing Augustine's description of the \nattenuated honour among thieves as an analogue for pagan justice, Thelaus \nwrote ofthe pirates that \"they respect between them a certain justice without \nwhich their society would be only brawls.\" This comment appears to echo \nAlexandre Exquemelin's description of buccaneer and pirate crews in the \nCaribbean organizing themselves according to strict codes of honour among \nthemselves.3? It is worth noting here that honour was also a principle that was \nalso invoked to convey a distinctly European understanding ofthe elaborate \nbut largely unwritten standards of conduct between states in order to ensure \ntrust.34 In the same year that Thelaus defended his dissertation, Francois \nde Calliéres (1645-1717) published what was to become a foundational text \nin European diplomacy, The Art of Negotiating With Princes. Calliéres spoke \nof the art of diplomacy as a delicate balance between deceit and honour \nnecessary to maintain the peace between “all the States of Europe” who \nmay “be look'd upon as Members of one and the same Commonwealth.”35 \nSignificantly, Calliéres remained focused on diplomatic relations between \nEuropean states. Many of those same states through a mix of economic \nnecessity and geopolitical interest, had long-established relations with the \nBarbary corsairs, and some among Europe's philosophers were prepared to \nconsider them at least as lawful combatants in war.°",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1645
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
