      {
        "finding_id": "section-f2019335b903c3a37413",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "humanity.\" Having argued himself back into the conventional condemnation \nof piracy, he concluded by citing the authority of a Dutch scholar, Van der \nMüelen (1635-1702), that extending diplomatic recognition to barbary pirates \nmay be expedient even though not properly respectable. On that basis, \nThelaus was prepared to ask whether it was \"possible to negotiate with the \nBarbary pirates?\" He seemed to concede the de facto autonomy ofthe Barbary \ncities and kingdoms from the Ottoman court. While formally part of a vast \nand powerful empire, the Barbary cities and kingdoms constituted a “form \nof state although aberrant and vitiated,” and though “not being a people\" \nunited in their observance of law and justice, they were still “united” if by \nnothing else than their shared “rascality.” Thelaus's casuistry led him into \na seeming paradox: piracy was undoubtedly an evil, yet the necessity of \ndiplomacy, war, and foreign trade required that expedience should perhaps \nbe allowed to trump natural law in certain circumstances.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1635
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
