      {
        "finding_id": "section-4fe4fe25b8de594e139b",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Just as Thelaus considered piracy from both a moral and pragmatic perspec- \ntive, so, too, did the Swedish Council of State and Secret Committee. In both \nthe scholarly dissertation and in the halls of power, the question of piracy \nencroached upon another to do with national honour. Would Sweden’s employ- \nment of pirates besmirch or enhance its honour? As Thelaus and the statesmen \nrecognized, the question of honour was inseparable from considerations of \nnational interest and both were entangled by the persistent image of the pirate \nas a faithless foe, “the scum of the earth.” There is no evidence that Thelaus or \nhis arguments were ever employed in service to the Swedish state. It is telling \nthat this one Swedish scholar was willing to address in a very deliberate way a \ndelicate question of state policy, almost in the same terms as Swedish officials \nwere later to rehearse. De piratica was thus a most unusual dissertation. It was \nprepared to concede intellectual cover to a desperate Swedish state forced by \nnecessity and interest to negotiate with the Barbary corsairs, and offer asylum \nto the Madagascar pirates, as desperate means to win back its fading empire.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
          "source_file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
