        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Etymologically, the word pirate can be traced to Marcus Tullius Cicero's writ- \nings in the first century BCE. Unlike earlier Greek words usually translated \nas piracy or pirates, such as léistés (Ayotys), the Latin word pirata only ever \nreferred to maritime marauders and not to robbers or brigands on land3 \nPirates, according to Cicero, were not subject to the Roman law of nations \n(jus gentium), according to which an oath sworn to a legal enemy must be \nkept: \"[A] pirate is not included in the number of lawful enemies, but is the",
        "years_in_scope": [],
