      {
        "finding_id": "section-68cc2c4bc0812ce38f49",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The surge in Chinese piracy in Vietnamese waters and in the South \nChina Sea and parts of Southeast Asia from the 1840s onwards was linked \nto the weakening of the Qing Dynasty in the wake of the Opium War and \nthe subsequent civil unrest in China, particularly the Taiping Rebellion \n(1850-1864). During the first years of British rule in Hong Kong in the 1840s, \nmoreover, the British government and the Royal Navy largely ignored the \nproblem of piracy, and corrupt officials and merchants in the colony even \ncolluded with the pirates.”5 From the middle of the century, however, the \nRoyal Navy began to take more oppressive measures against the pirates in \nthe vicinity of Hong Kong. From the 1860s, the Qing authorities regained \ncontrol over southern China and its coasts, and collaboration between \nthe British and Chinese to suppress piracy in and around China improved \nafter the end of the Arrow War. Around the same time, the British and \nDutch increased their efforts to stamp out piracy in and around the Strait \nof Malacca. Thus, pressured from both sides, many ofthe remaining pirates \nseem to have taken refuge in Vietnamese waters, where they met with \nlittle resistance from the authorities. Chinese and Vietnamese pirates thus \ncongregated in large numbers off the north Vietnamese coast, and many \nof them established permanent bases in the archipelago close to the Red \nRiver delta.”°",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
