      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-899493f9d20df3d515f1",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In May 1883, Riviére met a similar fate as Garnier at the hands of the \nBlack Flags. In contrast to what happened after Garnier was killed ten years \nearlier, however, Riviére’s death did not trigger a withdrawal of the French \ntroops. Instead, there was a massive outpouring of support in France for \na military intervention in Vietnam, in part because Riviére was not only \na soldier, but also a well-known author and journalist. Consequently, in \nParliament all but a few Socialists and Radicals came to strongly support \nthe plans for further colonial expansion in Vietnam. The need to suppress \npiracy - particularly with reference to the Black Flags — was invoked as a \nmajor reason for the intervention, but more fundamentally, the calls for",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
