      {
        "finding_id": "section-5622a4fccc7700569848",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Hugo Grotius and Alberico Gentili, for example, analysed legalities of the \nsea both in relation to natural law and as a function of limited but important \nrights to cast jurisdiction across sea space. In justifying the strike by a Dutch \ncaptain against the Portuguese ship Santa Catarina in the Indian Ocean, \nGrotius affirmed that the seas could not be owned, but he also allowed \nthat they could be lawfully controlled through the staging of ships on the \nsea. The presence of ships served to extend jurisdiction over particular \nstretches of ocean space, from proximate seas to jurisdictional corridors \nestablished “when a Fleet, which is a Sea-Army, is kept in any Part of the \nSea.”\" Grotius's description of jurisdiction on the seas echoed the writings of \nhis near-contemporary, Alberico Gentili, another jurist often credited with \nembedding Roman definitions of pirates as enemies of all into early modern \nEuropean jurisprudence. For Gentili, too, piracy was no easy \"jurisdictional \ntrigger\"? In his work as an advocate in admiralty cases, Gentili argued for \nthe recognition of jurisdiction by states over proximate seas and over the \nactions of subjects on ships far from home coasts. He offered contradictory \narguments about whether the capture of ships and cargoes by Barbary \nstates could be classed as piratical or defended as the actions of legitimate \npolities regulated by treaty.”",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
