          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Such episodes — and there are many more, in the British empire and in \nother empires — confound any assumption that a single European concept \nof piracy existed or that Europeans defaulted to definitions of piracy as \na universal crime. For most of its long history, piracy's regulation relied \non actions by states and courts reluctant to claim the right to exercise \nuniversal jurisdiction. Campaigns against \"pirates\" depended on an unstable \nmix of diplomacy, interpretations of treaties, regional political dynamics, \nand inter-imperial jockeying. Decisions were undoubtedly influenced by",
