        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Consider piracy's spatial dimensions. Gaynor points to the way references \nto piracy represented land-sea connections. She describes “the offing” as a \nspace literally and figuratively at the edge of the field of vision ofland-based \nauthorities and at the point where for mariners the unsurveilled ocean \nended.^ Spatial patterns of raiding reflected pirates’ positioning not beyond \nbut “at the limits of sovereignty.\"? Land-based labour practices structured \nseaborne capacity by releasing and reabsorbing men as part-time raiders. \nOther spatial patterns, such as the location and concentration of prize courts \nor the replication of coastal forts and townscapes designed to be visible",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
