      {
        "finding_id": "section-4c2e6ca523732f77204f",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "If we situate the labelling of pirates and their treatment squarely in the \nrealm of politics, then we do not have far to move to position the history of \npiracy within imperial legal politics, including imperial constitutionalism. \nConsider the central insight of Buchan's analysis of piracy in English political \nthought in this volume. Moving well beyond the usual story of a tradition \nof marking pirates as the enemies of all, Buchan notes that piracy came to \nsignify “a convenient analogue for illegitimate power over another.\"5 The \nlabelling of individuals as pirates affected not only the legitimacy of sea raid- \ners' sponsors but also the legality of arrangements delegating the authority \nto seize ships. Pirates might operate both as the *embodiment ofillegitimate \npower\" and as usurpers of legitimate sovereign power committing violence \n\"without sovereign sanction.\" The shorthand for the constitutional danger \nposed by these conditions together was petty despotism. The association of \npiracy and tyranny converted campaigns to control sea raiding in remote \nseas into constitutional crises, a pattern illustrated with particular clarity \nin British nineteenth century campaigns against piracy.^?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
