      {
        "finding_id": "section-0d7a73b7b5e459951d79",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "maneuvering for political autonomy. Historians toggle between portraying \npirates' experiments in self-governance as oppositional acts of stateless \nmen and characterizing communities of sea marauders as aspirational \nstates. Such alternatives are too limited.*? Maritime raiders fitted into \nbroader political patterns when they embraced a modicum of autonomy \nwithout necessarily opposing the power of empires. As several chapters \nin this volume affirm, even when sea raiders acted to found independent \npolities, the political arrangements they helped to craft were fluid, as they \nwere in the negotiations between Madagascar pirates and the Swedish \nKing.? Further, sea raiders not only routinely retained economic ties that \nbound them to powerful polities, but they also found it useful to rekindle \nclaims to “nested rights\" or to plead for protection — as individuals, clients \nof powerful states, or as allies within confederations.*?? Like others within \ncomposite political systems of the early modern world, mariners could \nreconcile visions of semi-autonomy and necessary acts of subordination.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
