      {
        "finding_id": "section-dc4614d2ef4d162e8716",
        "file": "oapen-20.500.12657-53019_external_content_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Piracy in World History",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-53019",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": null,
          "date": [
            "2021-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8f3c05c7515e4753fdbf1e6c89ebadf36b36a537f0572450eb8246a8822c8366",
        "evidence_excerpt": "As chapters in this volume show, such patterns made Portuguese and \nMaratha power, Chinese and European, and Spanish and Philippine *Moro\" \npower mutually legible, a condition that differed sharply from mutual \nacceptance. Truces and treaties tamped down raiding at intervals without \neliminating it. Commerce almost always continued alongside it. Religious \nsolidarities and differences shaped opportunities and outcomes. Recognizing \nthis complexity, Hágerdal notes that piracy covers a “broad spectrum of \nactivities\" and was a product of “a volatile intermixing of devastating war, \nweaponized religion, and aristocratic ambitions, in an archipelago offering \nmultiple opportunities for trade and profit.55 It was not, however, limitless \nin is varieties or disconnected from institutions. Raiding was a part of the \nlegal world of mariners and their sponsors. Bringing its rhythms into view \nplaces piracy and its politics within a truly globallegal panorama in which \nuniversal criminality and other set pieces of the history of international \nlaw figure on the margins.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
