      {
        "finding_id": "section-87028a784e36bce86e4f",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The  gallant  Spaniard,  it  is  to  be  hoped,  came  by \nhis  end  in  the  way  just  narrated ;  but  the  chroniclers \ndisagree  among  themselves,  and  \"  El  Sefior  Don \nDiego  de  Haedo,  Arcobispo  de  Palermo  y  Capitan \nGeneral  del  Reyno  de  Sicilia  por  El  Rey  Felipe \nnuestro  senor,\"  states  that  Barbarossa  kept  De \nVargas  in  confinement  for  three  months  and  then \nhad  him  beaten  to  death.  One  can  only  sincerely \nhope  that  the  first  account  is  the  true  one ;  but  Haedo \nwas  nearer  to  the  time  of  the  occurrence,  and,  as  he \nwrote  in  the  reign  of  Philip  II.,  is  more  likely  to  have \nknown  the  facts.  But  however  this  may  have  been, \nthere  was  an  end  for  all  time  of  Spanish  domination \non  the  north  coast  of  Africa,  and  from  this  we  may \ndate  the  permanent  establishment  of  those  piratical \nStates  in  that  part  of  the  world.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
