      {
        "finding_id": "section-b15e7e61fa36f8d3c4a9",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Accordingly,  reinforced  by  the  fifteen  fustas  and \none  galley  of  Delizuff,  the  Algerian  fleet  once  more \nproceeded  on  its  voyage.  Although  bound  for \nConstantinople  at  the  request  of  Soliman,  at  a  time \nwhen  it  would  have  been  thought  that  delay  was  not \nonly  dangerous  but  impolitic,  and  although  the \ncorsair  was  endeavouring  to  merge  the  pirate  in  the \nking  who  dealt  on  terms  of  equality  with  those  whom \nhe  now  regarded  as  his  brother  monarchs,  still  the \nold  instinct  of  robbery  was  too  strong  to  be  resisted  ; \nthe  lust  of  gain  and  the  call  of  adventure  were  still \ninherent  in  the  man  whose  famous  beard  was  now \nfar  more  white  than  red.  Advancing  age  had  not \ntamed  the  spirit  nor  weakened  the  frame  of  this \nleader  among  the  Moslems.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
