      {
        "finding_id": "section-eb1f2568611a20d66bea",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Thus  balked  of  his  prey,  Barbarossa  swung  his \nfleet  round  to  the  southward  and  westward  and \nsailed  for  Sardinia,  where,  from  the  Straits  of  Boni- \nfacio to  Cape  Spartivento,  he  left  no  house  standing \nthat  would  burn,  or  man  alive  who  was  not  swept \nin  as  a  captive.  The  descent  of  the  corsairs  in  force, \nsuch  as  Kheyr-ed-Din  now  had  at  his  disposal,  was \none  of  the  most  awful  calamities  for  a  country  that  it \nis  possible  to  imagine.  When  Sardinia  had  ceased \nto  yield  up  either  booty  or  slaves  the  fleet  sailed  for \nTunis,  where  it  arrived  before  Bizerta  on  August \n1 5th.  The  arrival  of  the  corsairs  was  totally  unex- \npected, and  caused  the  greatest  consternation.  The \nstory  which  Barbarossa  had  told  to  Sultan  Soliman \nconcerning  the  reigning  King  Muley  Hassan  was  cor- \nrect in  every  detail,  and  there  is  no  doubt  that  he",
