      {
        "finding_id": "section-90ead64b433248eebd22",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "and  larger  and  was  able  to  mount  more  and  more \npowerful  ordnance  ;  this  had  the  effect  of  discount- \ning the  value  of  the  galley  as  a  fighting  ship ;  in \nconsequence  she  became  practically  obsolete,  for \nthe  line  of  battle,  after  the  combat  at  Lepanto. \nIn  spite  of  this  she  was  to  linger  on  for  many  long \nyears  to  come  as  the  weapon  of  the  corsairs  who \nhad  established  themselves  on  the  coast  of  Africa. \nThe  \"  long  ship  \"  was  still  to  be  the  cause  of  many \nan  awful  sea  tragedy,  whether  the  actors  therein \nwere  the  pirates  who  hailed  from  the  Barbary  coast \nor  their  most  capable  imitators  the  notorious  rovers \nof  Sallee.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
